export const articles: Article[] = [
{ slug: "ats-resume-guide", guide: true, title: "ATS Resume Checker Pakistan: How the Software Reads and Ranks Your CV", desc: "Free ATS resume checker Pakistan with a full guide on how applicant tracking systems read your CV and what gets people rejected plus how to fix it free.", intro: "A young man from Rawalpindi spent eight months chasing a job in Saudi Arabia and heard nothing back from anyone. His CV carried his CNIC number and his father name and his full home address while his four years of real store work sat at the bottom in two thin lines. We rebuilt that CV on the Gulf format in one evening and within six weeks two Saudi companies called him for interviews.", sections: [
 { h: "What an ATS Actually Is in Plain Words", p: ["Quick answer. An ATS is software companies use to collect and sort and search the CVs that arrive for a post. It reads your file and turns it into data. It ranks you against the job before a human opens the shortlist.", "ATS stands for Applicant Tracking System. The long name scares people more than the thing itself deserves.", "Think of it as the office clerk of a very large company. When five hundred people apply for one post no manager can read five hundred CVs with fresh eyes. So the company hands the pile to this clerk first. The clerk opens every file and copies the important parts into neat boxes. Name here. Phone here. Education here. Experience here. Skills here. Then the manager searches the boxes. Show me everyone with three years of accounts experience. Show me everyone who knows Excel. The clerk shows only the files whose boxes were filled correctly.", "Here is the part nobody tells you. The clerk is fast and tireless and completely literal. He cannot guess. He cannot admire your design. He cannot read words locked inside a picture. If your skills are written inside an image he copies nothing into the skills box. As far as the company is concerned you have no skills.", "Small companies and shops and most Pakistani offices still hire the old way. Human eyes on paper or on a phone screen. Large companies and banks and telecoms and multinationals and Gulf recruiters and every big job portal use some form of this software. If you apply to big doors you are applying to the clerk first. This guide is written for those doors."] },
 { h: "How the Software Reads Your CV Step by Step", p: ["Quick answer. The software opens your file and pulls out the text. It tries to sort that text into sections and fields. Then it matches those fields against the words in the job advertisement. Whatever it cannot sort it quietly drops.", "The reading happens in three passes. Each pass has its own way of losing you.", "The first pass is extraction. The machine pulls raw text out of your file. This is where designed CVs start bleeding. Text inside images does not extract at all. Text inside tables extracts in the order the cells sit and not in the order a human reads them. Text in headers and footers sometimes extracts twice or not at all. Two column layouts extract as one long mixed river where the left column and the right column pour into each other line by line.", "The second pass is sorting. The machine looks for signposts it recognizes. It hunts for the word Education and the word Experience and the word Skills because those are the signposts it was built around. When it finds your phone near your name it fills the contact box. When it finds dates beside a job title it builds your work history. If your headings are creative instead of standard — My Journey or Where I Have Been — the machine finds no signpost. Your experience may land in the wrong box or in no box at all.", "The third pass is matching. The job advertisement carries the words the company cares about. Job titles and tools and skills and qualifications. The machine compares your sorted boxes against those words and ranks the pile. This is why two men with the same real experience finish in different positions. One wrote his experience in the words of the advertisement. The other wrote the same work in his own private words and the machine never connected them.", "Different systems behave a little differently from each other. Some read tables better than others. Some skip headers completely and some do not. But the failure points above are the common ones across almost every system in use today.", "None of this is personal. It is a literal reader doing a literal job. Your task is to write for two readers at once. The machine first and the human second. The good news is that what the machine loves — plain text and standard headings and honest keywords — is almost exactly what a busy human reader loves too."] },
 { h: "The Five Things That Break Inside a Designed CV", p: ["Quick answer. Images of text. Tables. Text boxes. Headers carrying your contact details. Multi column layouts with mixed reading order. These five classic breakers can make a CV beautiful and broken at the same time.", "I check CVs on our tool every week and the same five wounds return again and again. Count how many live inside your file right now.", "One. Text locked inside images. Skill bars and icons and logos and sidebar graphics that carry words as pictures. The machine reads pictures as blank space. If your ten skills are a graphic then your file officially has zero skills.", "Two. Tables holding your real content. Many Word templates park experience and education inside table cells. Some systems read tables acceptably. Many read them cell by cell in an order that scrambles dates away from jobs and marks away from degrees.", "Three. Text boxes and floating shapes. The pretty designs from design tools love floating boxes. Extraction often drops them entirely or reads them at the very end of the file where your summary suddenly appears after your references.", "Four. Contact details in the header or footer. A surprising number of systems skip headers and footers by design. A CV whose phone number lives only in the header is a CV with no phone number as far as the shortlist is concerned.", "Five. Columns that mix. A two column CV can be built safely when one column holds only contact and skills and the text stays real — our own templates include such options for markets that accept them. The dangerous kind pours experience text across columns or wraps lines between them. The machine reads your jobs in a scrambled order no human would ever speak.", "Notice the pattern. Every breaker is a design decision that treated the CV as a poster. A CV is not a poster. It is a document that must survive being read by a machine at two in the morning. Build for that reader first and add your beauty inside safe borders."] },
 { h: "Headings and Words the Machine Is Hunting For", p: ["Quick answer. Use standard heading words the software was built around. Summary. Experience. Education. Skills. Use the honest words of the job advertisement for the work you truly did. Standard signposts plus true keywords is the whole secret.", "There is no magic list and no hidden setting. The machine hunts for two ordinary things and most CVs fail on both out of pure creativity.", "The first is signposts. Write your section headings in plain standard words. Experience or Work Experience. Education. Skills. Summary or Professional Summary. Languages. Certifications. These words are boring on purpose. They are the exact words the sorting pass expects. Every creative replacement is a small gamble with your own shortlist. Put your name in large letters at the top. Put your phone and email directly under it in the body of the page and not in the header. Write dates in a simple month and year shape beside every post so the machine can build your timeline without guessing.", "The second is true keywords. Read the job advertisement the way the machine will. It names a job title and a set of tools and skills and qualifications. If you honestly hold those things then your CV should carry those exact words in your summary and your skills and your experience lines. The data entry post asks for data entry and typing speed and MS Excel. If your page says computer work and office tasks instead then the match is weaker even though a human knows you mean the same thing. The machine does not know. Say the words.", "One limit matters here and it protects you. Use only the words that are true for you. Keyword stuffing — hiding word lists in white text or pasting the advertisement into your footer — is read as fraud by modern systems and by any human who spots it. The goal is translation and not decoration. Translate your real work into the market words for that work. Our complete [CV format in Pakistan](https://cvmakerpakistan.com/cv-format-pakistan) guide shows that translation inside a full format with a sample."] },
 { h: "PDF or Word for an ATS Friendly Resume", p: ["Quick answer. Send the file the advertisement asks for. When it asks for neither a text based PDF is the safe default because it holds your layout steady on every machine. Never send a scanned image PDF because the machine reads it as a photograph.", "This question reaches me every week and the honest answer has two doors.", "If the advertisement or the portal names a format then send exactly that format and ask no further questions. Some older systems genuinely parse Word files more kindly. Some portals demand Word for their own editing. Obey the door you are knocking on.", "If nothing is named then send a PDF whose text is real and selectable. Open your own file and try to select a line with your mouse or your finger. If the words highlight the text is alive and the machine can read it. If the whole page moves as one picture your CV is a photograph and the clerk will file it as blank. This ten second test has saved more applications than any paid service I know.", "CVs built on our own [free CV maker](https://cvmakerpakistan.com/resume-builder) print to PDF with real selectable text and standard headings for exactly this reason. Your data stays in your browser while you build and nothing is uploaded anywhere.", "One more file habit that quietly matters. Name your file like a professional document and not like a phone download. Your name plus the word CV is the shape recruiters save and search. A file called final final new 2 pdf tells its own sad story before it is even opened."] },
 { h: "The Truth About ATS Scores and Keywords", p: ["Quick answer. No checker on earth can promise the score a company system will give because every company configures its own machine differently. A checker is a mirror for spotting broken reading and missing words. It is not a crystal ball.", "Since I run a checker myself let me tell you the truth that checker sellers hide.", "Every employer sets up its software its own way. One company weights the job title heavily. Another searches only for three named tools. A third uses the system as a filing cabinet and ranks almost nothing. The same CV can be a hero at one door and invisible at another and both readings are the software working exactly as configured. That is why our own checker caps its score at 95 and says so openly on the page. No honest tool can hand you 100 because there is no single machine to score 100 against.", "So what is a checker actually for? Three things. It shows you what a machine managed to extract from your file so broken layouts stop hiding from you. It compares your words against the words of a trade or a post so missing keywords stop hiding from you. It forces the boring checks — phone present and email present and headings standard and file readable — before a real shortlist teaches you the same lessons at full price.", "Run the check. Fix what it shows. Then stop polishing the score and go back to the only ranking input that has ever mattered. Real experience written in the words of the job by a person who truly did the work. Our free [ATS resume checker](https://cvmakerpakistan.com/ats-resume-checker) does this check without sign up and your file is read in your own browser session."] },
 { h: "A Worked Example. One Bullet Before and After", p: ["Quick answer. The machine does not hate your experience. It hates vague words. One honest bullet rewritten with the job title word and the tool word and a number shows you the entire method in ten seconds.", "Watch one real shape change and you will understand this whole guide.", "Before. Responsible for data entry and computer work in the office.", "That line is true and almost invisible. It carries no tool name and no speed and no volume. The word responsible is the emptiest word in CV writing because it claims a duty and proves nothing.", "After. Data entry operator. Entered 400+ records daily into MS Excel and the company portal with an accuracy rate checked weekly by the supervisor.", "Same man. Same chair. Same work. The second line carries the market title and the tool and a daily quantity and a proof habit. The machine can match it and a human can believe it and an interviewer can ask about it without either of you wasting the first five minutes.", "Do this translation to every weak line on your page. Duty word out. Tool word in. Number in. Proof in. It is slow work for one evening and it is the entire difference between the Karachi operator who was rejected by a clerk made of software and the same operator who got the call two months later."] },
 { h: "How to Check If Your CV Is ATS Friendly", p: ["Quick answer. Do the copy paste test and the ten second file test and one run through a checker. If your CV survives all three it will survive the clerk at almost any big door.", "You do not need paid software to protect yourself. You need three small rituals and ten honest minutes.", "The copy paste test comes first. Open your CV file and copy all of its text and paste it into a plain notes page. Now read what arrived. If the order is scrambled and your skills appear after your references and your education is mixed into your jobs then you are reading exactly what many machines will read. Fix the layout until the pasted text reads like the document you meant to send.", "The file test comes second. Select one line of text with your finger or your mouse. If words highlight one by one your text is alive. If the page behaves like one photograph rebuild the file as real text before it goes anywhere near a portal.", "The checker run comes third. Put the file through our free ATS checker and read what it managed to find. Your correct phone. Your correct email. Your jobs in the right order. Your skills present as words. Whatever the checker cannot find the company clerk probably cannot find either. Now you know it while fixing is still free.", "Do these three and you have done more for your applications than any keyword trick on the internet. Machines reward readable honesty. They always have."] },
 { h: "Mistakes That Get Qualified People Rejected by Software", p: ["Quick answer. A designed CV with text in images. Creative headings no machine recognizes. Contact details hiding in a header. Vague duty words instead of the words of the job. The wrong file sent to the wrong door. These are what get qualified people rejected.", "I read rejected CVs every week and the same wounds return on page after page. Count how many of them are sitting on your CV right now.", "1. A designed poster CV sent to a portal. Beautiful on Instagram and blank inside the machine", "2. Creative section headings. My Journey and My Toolbox and Where I Shine are poetry to you and silence to the sorting pass", "3. Phone and email living in the header or footer where many systems never look", "4. Vague duty words everywhere. Responsible for and worked in and handled without one tool name or number on the whole page", "5. A scanned or photographed CV sent as the application file. The machine files it as an empty page with total confidence", "6. Keywords copied from the advertisement that you do not actually hold. The machine may pass you and the interview will end you", "7. One CV sent to every door unchanged. The words of a bank post and the words of a Gulf post and the words of a software house post are three different languages", "8. An old CV with a dead phone number or an email you never open. The machine did its job perfectly and the human call went to a number that no longer rings", "None of these need money or a paid service to fix. They need one honest evening and the format in this guide."] },
 { h: "A Five Minute Checklist Before You Apply Anywhere", p: ["Quick answer. Seven boxes. Real selectable text. Standard headings. Contact details in the body. The words of the job where they are true for you. The file format the door asked for. One checker run survived. Every claim able to survive an interview.", "Before any application leaves your phone or laptop run through this list once. Five minutes of checking can protect months of silence.", "- My CV text is real and selectable and the copy paste test reads in the right order", "- My headings are the standard words. Summary and Experience and Education and Skills", "- My phone and email sit in the body under my name and both are typed correctly", "- My experience lines carry tool names and numbers and the honest words of the job I want", "- I am sending the file format this door asked for and the file name carries my name and the word CV", "- I have run the file through a checker once and fixed what it could not find", "- Nothing on this page is copied or invented and I can defend every line in an interview", "If all seven boxes tick true your CV will be read correctly at almost any big door in Pakistan or the Gulf. For the Pakistan format behind the words themselves read the complete [CV format in Pakistan](https://cvmakerpakistan.com/cv-format-pakistan) guide. If the Gulf is your target the [CV for Gulf and Saudi jobs](https://cvmakerpakistan.com/cv-for-gulf-saudi-jobs) guide bends this same safe format for that reader."] },
 { h: "Final Words for the Candidates Reading This", p: ["The machine is not your enemy and it is not going away. Every year more Pakistani companies hand their first reading to software. Every year more good candidates lose posts they never knew they were rejected from.", "The operator from Karachi did not become a better operator in the evening we rebuilt his CV. He became a readable one. His three years were always there. They were just locked inside a design that the reader of his file could not open.", "Take one evening before your next application. Run the three tests. Translate your duties into tools and numbers. Then apply with a calm heart and let both of your readers — the machine and the human — finally see the same true page.", "One small request before you go. If this guide helped you share it with one friend who keeps getting silence from big companies. It takes ten seconds and it helps this free guide reach the people who need it most."] },
 { h: "A Short Note From the Writer", p: ["My name is Muhammad Kashif and I built the free CV maker and the free ATS resume checker on this site after watching good candidates around me lose interviews to bad formatting and silent software. I set our own checker to cap its score at 95 and to say so openly because honesty about what a machine can promise is the first rule of this whole topic. Almost every rule in this guide comes from a real CV that a real system failed to read. I am not a recruiter. I am the person who fixes the document before the machine and the manager see it and this guide is written from exactly that chair."] },
], faq: [{ q: "What is an ATS and why does it reject CVs?", a: "An ATS is the software companies use to collect and sort applications before humans read the shortlist. It does not reject you out of judgement. It extracts the text of your file and sorts it into fields and matches those fields against the job words. If your text is locked in images or tables or scrambled columns then the extraction fails and your sorted record looks empty even when your real experience is strong." }, { q: "What is a good ATS score for a CV?", a: "There is no universal good score because every company configures its own system differently and the same CV scores differently at different doors. Treat any checker score as a repair list and not as a verdict. If the checker found your contact details and your jobs and your skills correctly and your words match the job honestly then you have done what is yours to do." }, { q: "Should I send my CV as PDF or Word?", a: "Send the format the advertisement or the portal asks for. When nothing is named a text based PDF is the safe default because it holds your layout on every machine and its text stays selectable. Never send a scanned image PDF to a portal because the software reads it as a photograph and files your application as blank." }, { q: "Can a two column CV pass an ATS?", a: "It depends on how the columns are built. A two column layout where one narrow column holds only contact details and skills in real text and the main column holds your story in clean order can pass many systems. The dangerous kind mixes experience text across columns or carries words inside graphics. When the door is a big portal and you are unsure a single column version is the safer file to send." }, { q: "How do I know if my CV is ATS friendly?", a: "Run three tests. Copy all the text and paste it into a plain notes page and check that the order reads correctly. Select a line and confirm the words highlight as real text. Then run the file through a free ATS checker and confirm it found your phone and your email and your jobs and your skills. A CV that passes all three is friendly to almost every system you will meet." }, { q: "Why is my CV not getting shortlisted in Pakistan?", a: "Most of the time it is not your experience. It is the file shape. A designed layout with images and tables and a header full of contact details gets read wrong by the software before a human ever opens the shortlist. Fix the format first with the tests in this guide and your real experience finally gets a fair read." }] },
{ slug: "resume-format-guide", guide: true, title: "Resume Format Guide: Chronological, Functional or Hybrid", desc: "Compare the three resume formats and choose the layout that fits your experience, with ATS readability in mind.", intro: "Your format controls what a recruiter sees first. The best resume format is the one that puts your strongest, most relevant evidence at the top.", sections: [
 { h: "Reverse-chronological", p: ["Lists jobs from newest to oldest. It is the default for most roles and the easiest for an ATS to parse. Choose it if your career history is steady and relevant."] },
 { h: "Functional (skills-based)", p: ["Groups achievements by skill instead of by job. It can hide gaps, but recruiters tend to distrust it and parsers struggle to link skills to employers. Use it rarely."] },
 { h: "Hybrid (combination)", p: ["Opens with a short skills or achievements block, then lists jobs in reverse order. It suits career changers and developers who want tools visible above the fold."] },
 { h: "Margins, fonts and length", p: ["Use 1.5-2 cm margins, 10-12 pt body text and one page per ten years of experience as a rough guide. Stick to one or two typefaces and keep headings consistent."] }],
 faq: [{ q: "Which resume format is best for ATS?", a: "Reverse-chronological is the safest for ATS. It lists jobs newest first with standard headings, so parsers can match employers, dates and skills without guessing." }, { q: "Can I use a functional format if I have a career gap?", a: "You can, but recruiters often distrust it because it hides dates. A hybrid format is usually better: open with a short skills block, then list jobs honestly with dates." }, { q: "How long should a hybrid CV be?", a: "Fresh graduates should keep it to 1 page. Experienced candidates can use 2 pages if the second page carries strong, relevant achievements." }] },
{ slug: "what-is-an-ats-resume", title: "What Is an ATS Resume?", desc: "An ATS resume is one an applicant tracking system can read. Here is what that means and what it does not.", intro: "There is no special file type called an ATS resume (or ATS CV). The phrase just means a resume built so software can extract its text accurately.", sections: [
 { h: "How it differs from a normal resume", p: ["The content is the same: experience, education, skills. The difference is structure. It is single-column, uses standard headings and keeps key details as real text."] },
 { h: "What it does not mean", p: ["It does not mean plain or ugly. A clean template with good spacing and an accent colour parses fine. It also does not guarantee an interview, because people make the final decision."] },
 { h: "What an ATS-friendly heading structure looks like", p: ["Use these exact headings so software can sort your details: Summary, Experience, Education, Skills, Certifications. Avoid creative headings such as My Journey, What I Bring, or Stuff I Am Good At — parsers often do not recognise them and may drop that whole section.", "Keep contact details as normal text in the body of the page: full name, phone, email and city. Do not put them only in a header, footer, text box or image, because some parsers skip those areas."] },
 { h: "Before and after: a bullet that parses vs one that does not", p: ["Hard to parse: a bullet written inside a coloured text box with an icon instead of a bullet character, plus the date hidden in a table cell. The parser may read it out of order or skip it entirely.", "Easy to parse: a plain bullet that reads: Managed a team of 4 sales assistants and raised monthly store revenue by 12% in 6 months. Same achievement, but now it is real text in a single column with a clear date beside the job title."] },
 { h: "How to build one", p: ["Start from an ATS-friendly template, fill in tailored content, then test it with a checker before sending."] },
 { h: "Quick test before you send", p: ["Open your saved PDF and try to select the text with your mouse. If you can select it, the file is text-based and readable. If you cannot, it is an image-only file and most ATS software cannot read it. Also check that your name, latest job and skills appear in the right order when you copy all the text into a plain text file."] }],
 faq: [{ q: "Do I need a special ATS template?", a: "You do not need a special file type. Any clean, single-column template with standard headings and real text works. Start from an ATS-friendly template, then fill it with tailored content." }, { q: "Can a designed CV still pass ATS?", a: "Yes, if the design stays simple: one column, standard headings, real text and no text inside images. Heavy graphics, icons for contact details and two-column layouts are what break parsing, not colour or spacing." }, { q: "How do I test if my CV is ATS-readable?", a: "Open the PDF and try selecting the text. If it selects, the file is text-based. Then paste the text into a plain document and check that your name, jobs, dates and skills appear in the right order." }] },
{ slug: "write-resume-bullet-points", title: "How to Write Resume Bullet Points That Get Read", desc: "A simple formula for resume bullet points, with before-and-after examples and tips for adding numbers.", intro: "Resume bullet points are where recruiters decide whether you did the work or just attended. Each one should show an action and a result.", sections: [
 { h: "Use action + task + result", p: ["Start with a strong verb, say what you did, then state the outcome. Weak: Responsible for reports. Stronger: Built weekly sales reports that cut review time by 30%."] },
 { h: "Add numbers when you honestly can", p: ["Use percentages, money, team size, volume or time saved. If you lack exact figures, use a defensible estimate and be ready to explain it in an interview."] },
 { h: "Before and after examples", p: ["Teacher — Before: Responsible for teaching students. After: Raised class reading scores by 18% in one academic year by switching to project-based reading tasks.", "Developer — Before: Worked on website performance. After: Cut page load time from 3.8 seconds to 1.9 seconds by removing unused scripts and compressing images.", "Fresh graduate — Before: Helped organise a university event. After: Organised a charity event with 120 attendees that raised 1,500 USD for the computer lab.", "In each case the job is the same. The difference is a verb, a task and a measurable result."] },
 { h: "Common bullet mistakes", p: ["Starting with Responsible for or Duties included turns a bullet into a job description. Starting with a verb turns it into proof.", "Listing ten generic duties for one role buries the two achievements that matter. Keep three to five bullets per recent role and drop duties anyone in the role would have.", "Stuffing buzzwords without proof — fast learner, team player, hard worker — says nothing. Show the same qualities through a result instead."] },
 { h: "Keep it short", p: ["Aim for one or two lines per bullet and three to five bullets per recent role. Drop duties that any person in the role would have."] }],
 faq: [{ q: "How many bullets should each job have?", a: "Three to five bullets for recent and relevant roles, one to two for older or less relevant ones. Every bullet should show an action and a result." }, { q: "What if I have no numbers for my work?", a: "Use team size, volume, time saved, or how often you did the task. If you truly have no figures, describe the result in words — for example, reduced customer complaints after rewriting the returns process — and be ready to explain it." }, { q: "Should bullets start with I or my?", a: "No. Start with a past-tense verb: Built, Managed, Raised, Cut, Organised. Skip pronouns to keep bullets short and scannable." }] },
{ slug: "how-long-should-a-resume-be", title: "How Long Should a Resume Be?", desc: "One page or two? A practical guide to resume length by career stage.", intro: "How long should a resume be? Length matters less than relevance, but there are sensible limits.", sections: [
 { h: "Students and early career", p: ["One page is almost always enough. Lead with education, projects and internships."] },
 { h: "Experienced professionals", p: ["Two pages are acceptable with ten or more years of relevant experience. Fill the second page only with strong content."] },
 { h: "Length by role: a quick guide", p: ["Fresh graduate or student: 1 page. One to five years of experience: 1 page, maximum 2. More than five years: up to 2 pages. Senior managers and academics: 2 pages, or longer only for a full academic CV with publications.", "Pakistan and Gulf employers follow the same pattern: freshers 1 page, experienced candidates up to 2 pages. A longer CV does not impress anyone if the extra page is filler."] },
 { h: "What to cut first", p: ["Start with roles older than 10 to 15 years, then repeated duties, then hobbies, a full street address, and references listed in full. References can be Available on request.", "Next, cut any bullet that does not support the job you want. If a line would fit any applicant for any job, it is not earning its space."] },
 { h: "What never to cut", p: ["Never cut your contact details, your most recent job, or the skills the job advertisement asks for. If the posting names a tool or certification you have, that line stays even if the page gets tight."] },
 { h: "How to cut", p: ["Remove roles older than 10-15 years, merge repeated duties and delete objective statements that say nothing specific."] }],
 faq: [{ q: "Is a 2-page CV okay for fresh graduates?", a: "Usually no. Recruiters expect a concise 1-page CV from fresh graduates. A second page with weak filler hurts more than it helps." }, { q: "Does a longer CV improve my chances?", a: "No. Recruiters skim. A dense 3-page CV gets less attention than a sharp 1-page CV. Every line should support the job you want." }, { q: "What is the difference between a CV and a resume?", a: "In most job applications the words are used interchangeably. A full academic CV can run longer with publications, while a job resume should stay at 1 to 2 pages." }] },
{ slug: "resume-mistakes-that-hurt-ats-readability", title: "Resume Mistakes That Can Hurt ATS Readability", desc: "Common formatting mistakes that cause parsing errors, and how to fix each.", intro: "Most ATS readability problems come from design choices, not content.", sections: [
 { h: "Layout mistakes", p: ["Two-column layouts, text boxes and tables can be read out of order. Use a single column."] },
 { h: "Content mistakes", p: ["Creative headings like My Journey may not be recognised. Use Experience, Education and Skills. Putting contact details only in the header can also lose them."] },
 { h: "File mistakes", p: ["Scanned PDFs and photos of resumes contain no readable text. Export a text-based PDF and try selecting the text to confirm."] },
 { h: "Quick self-check before you send", p: ["Run through this list: 1) The PDF text selects with your mouse. 2) The layout is one column. 3) Headings are Summary, Experience, Education, Skills. 4) Dates use one format everywhere. 5) Contact details are in the body as real text. 6) No key detail lives only inside an image, icon or text box. If all six pass, your CV is in good shape for most parsers."] },
 { h: "Mistakes that cost interviews, not just parsing", p: ["A typo in your own name, email or phone number loses the interview even if parsing is perfect. Read those three lines twice.", "Claiming skills you cannot demonstrate fails at the interview. List only what you can show.", "Sending the same generic CV to every job wastes the one page you have. Mirror the posting's honest keywords for each application instead."] }],
 faq: [{ q: "Why does my CV look fine but the ATS reads it wrong?", a: "The usual cause is layout: two columns, text boxes, tables, or text inside images. The design looks fine to a human eye, but the parser reads the text out of order or skips it." }, { q: "Can I use a photo on an ATS CV?", a: "A photo does not help parsing and is not expected in the US, UK or Netherlands. In Pakistan and Gulf countries, add one only if the job advertisement asks for it — and keep it small so the layout stays single-column. Note: this builder does not add photos — if one is required, add it in your document tool before printing." }, { q: "Should I submit PDF or Word to avoid parsing errors?", a: "Submit a text-based PDF unless the employer asks for Word. Both usually parse fine. What matters is real, selectable text in a single column with standard headings." }] },

{ slug: "cv-format-pakistan", guide: true, title: "Best CV Format in Pakistan: How to Write a CV With a Full Sample", desc: "Learn the correct CV format in Pakistan with the right section order and real education examples plus sector tips. Build your CV free and print it to PDF.", intro: "Last year a young man from Faisalabad came to me with a strange problem. He had good marks and a degree from a known university and more than forty applications behind him. Not one company called him back. The problem was his CV format and this guide is the full format we rebuilt for him in one evening.", sections: [
 { h: "A Good Candidate With a Broken Format", p: ["Last year a young man from Faisalabad came to me with a strange problem.", "He had good marks. He had a degree from a known university. He had applied to more than forty jobs. Not one company called him back.", "He showed me his CV on his phone and I saw the reason in ten seconds. His whole life story was spread over four pages. His matric result was missing. His phone number was typed wrong on page one and right on page four. And his CV started with a long paragraph about his family background.", "We rebuilt that CV in one evening. Same marks. Same degree. Same boy. Two weeks later he had three interview calls. Three calls from companies that had stayed completely silent for months and I wish I could say that this surprised me but it honestly did not surprise me at all.", "I run a free CV maker and I see this same story repeat almost every single day and in almost every such case the candidate himself is completely fine while it is only the format of his CV that is broken. This guide is the full format I wish someone had handed him on day one. Read it slowly and fix your CV side by side. By the end your CV will look the way Pakistani recruiters expect to see it."] },
 { h: "The Standard Order of a Pakistani CV", p: ["Why does this order matter so much that I am starting the whole guide with it? Because a recruiter gives your CV roughly ten seconds on the first pass and those ten seconds quietly decide everything that happens to you next. Do not think of a Pakistani CV as your life story because nobody on the hiring side reads it that way and honestly they do not have the time for that. Think of it as a form where every block has its fixed place and the reader runs his eyes down the page in that same fixed order looking for the block he needs next.", "The moment a section sits in the wrong place the poor reader has to stop and hunt for it and hunting is exactly what a tired recruiter with two hundred files on his desk will never do for you. He simply moves on to the next CV in the pile and your chance ends right there without a single word said to anyone.", "Here is the order that works.", "1. Your full name and the job title you want", "2. Phone number and email and city", "3. A short career objective or profile summary of three to four lines", "4. Education written from the newest degree down to matric", "5. Work experience written from the newest job down to the oldest", "6. Skills that match the job you are applying for", "7. Certifications and short courses", "8. Languages", "9. References or one honest line saying references are available on request", "That order works for almost every private job in Pakistan. Government jobs are a little different and I will come to them later in this guide.", "People search for this same thing with many different words. CV template in Pakistan. Professional CV in Pakistan. How to write a CV for a job application in Pakistan. The words change from person to person. The document they all need does not. It is the format on this page.", "Notice what is not in the list. There is no long personal paragraph at the top. There is no family history. There is no list of hobbies from class eight.", "So keep the order boring and do not try to make it clever or different. Boring is exactly what gets read when a desk is full of files."] },
 { h: "Personal Information: What Goes In and What Stays Out", p: ["Old Pakistani CVs carried a big personal block. Father name. Date of birth. Domicile. Religion. Marital status. Some of that still matters here and some of it now hurts you.", "Father name still belongs on a Pakistani CV. Companies and government departments cross check it with your CNIC at joining time and a spelling mismatch creates real delays. Write your name and your father name exactly as they are printed on your CNIC. One letter of difference is enough to stall a joining file and I have seen a joining sit stuck for days over exactly one such letter while the poor candidate ran between offices with his file in his hand.", "Domicile matters for government quota jobs so keep it when you apply there. For private jobs in the same city it adds very little.", "Now the part where I disagree with many template sites. Never print your full CNIC number on a CV that you send to strangers and upload to job portals. That number is your identity. Think about it for a second with a cool head. You would not hand your house keys to every stranger who knocks at the door so why hand him this? A name and a city are enough for shortlisting. Show the card when a real employer asks at the hiring stage.", "Skip religion and marital status unless the job ad asks for them. They do not change your ability to do the job and some multinational firms prefer CVs without them.", "One clean photo rule. Private IT and multinational jobs usually need no photo. Government and sales and front desk roles often expect one. When you add a photo use a plain head and shoulders shot with a clean background. No selfies. No wedding pictures cropped at the last minute."] },
 { h: "How to Write Matric and FSc So a Recruiter Understands Them", p: ["This is the section foreign templates always get wrong and Pakistani recruiters always look for.", "In Pakistan your education does not stop at your degree. Employers here read your full ladder. Degree. Intermediate. Matric. A CV that hides matric looks incomplete to a local reader even when the degree is strong. Our mothers keep our matric sheets safe in plastic folders at home for decades and Pakistani employers honestly think in a very similar way about that sheet.", "Write every level in this shape.", "- BBA. University of the Punjab. 2023. CGPA 3.4 out of 4", "- FSc Pre Engineering. BISE Lahore. 2019. 912 marks out of 1100 (83%)", "- Matric Science. BISE Lahore. 2017. 945 marks out of 1100 (86%)", "Marks out of the total matter here. A line that only says first division tells a modern recruiter almost nothing. A line with marks and percentage and board can be checked and trusted in seconds.", "FSc confuses many freshers. Write the full group name the way your board prints it. FSc Pre Medical. FSc Pre Engineering. ICS. ICom. FA. Do not invent a short form of your own and do not hide the group because you think the reader will guess it. He will not guess. He will pass.", "If your marks in one level are weak do not delete that level. Recruiters notice the gap and assume the worst. A complete honest ladder beats a broken one every single time."] },
 { h: "Work Experience That Sounds Real", p: ["A salesman from Multan once showed me a CV where his entire experience of six years was written in one single line. One line for six years of work. When I asked him what he actually did all day he talked for twenty minutes without stopping even once. His work was rich and full. His line was poor and empty. That gap between the work and the line is exactly what this section exists to fix.", "Most experience sections fail in one simple way. They list duties that anyone could claim.", "A line like responsible for sales tells the reader nothing. He does not know if you sold ten units or ten thousand units. He does not know if the shop had two customers a day or two hundred.", "Every strong bullet carries a number. Rupees. Customers. Files. Students. Machines. Whatever your job honestly produced.", "- Weak: Worked as a teacher in a school", "- Strong: Taught mathematics to 180 students of classes nine and ten and raised the board pass rate of my section from 71% to 88% in one year", "- Weak: Handled accounts work in an office", "- Strong: Managed daily cash entries for a textile unit with monthly turnover of Rs 4500000 and closed the month with zero pending vouchers", "Freshers worry here the most. You do have experience. Internships count. Final year projects count. Freelance work and family business work count when you describe them honestly with numbers.", "Never copy bullets from the internet word for word. Recruiters read hundreds of CVs and they smell a copied line in seconds. Your numbers are your protection. Nobody else on earth has your exact numbers."] },
 { h: "Skills: The Small Section Most People Fill Wrong", p: ["Skills sections fail in two opposite ways. Some people list twenty skills they half know. Some list three skills and hide the rest inside long paragraphs where nobody finds them.", "Write six to ten skills that this job actually needs. Put the strongest ones first. Every skill on your list is a promise you must defend in the interview room so never write a skill just because it looks impressive on paper.", "Split them in your head before you write. Job skills first. Computer skills next. Language skills last with honest levels written in plain words.", "Never rate your own skills with stars or bars or percentages of your own invention. A line that says spoken English good and written English strong tells a manager far more than five golden stars ever will. And one small request from my side. Please stop writing MS Word as a proud skill in this day and age unless the job ad itself asks for it by name. Knowing it is assumed the same way knowing how to use a phone is assumed."] },
 { h: "Formats by Sector: One Size Does Not Fit Every Job", p: ["The order above is your base. After that each sector bends the format a little. Here is what I tell people from the sectors I hear from most.", "**Fresh Graduates and Students**", "Keep it to one page. Nobody hires a fresher for a long story.", "Put education first and skills right after it. Your projects and internships go where experience would normally sit. A short objective line helps here because the reader needs to know what you are aiming at before he judges what you have done so far. If you are a fresher read the separate guide on [CV format for freshers in Pakistan](https://cvmakerpakistan.com/cv-for-freshers-pakistan) for a one page plan built only for you.", "**Teachers and Lecturers**", "Schools read your subjects before anything else. Name the classes and subjects you can teach in your summary line itself.", "Add your BEd or MEd clearly if you have it. Board results of your past classes are gold on a teacher CV. Academic CVs for lecturer posts can run longer because publications and research honestly belong there.", "**Banking and Office Jobs**", "Banks and corporate offices like a formal two page format with clean education lines and a sober design. They are strict people in strict buildings and they judge small carelessness very quickly.", "Computer skills deserve their own lines here. Excel and typing speed and any banking or accounting software you know should never hide inside one crowded paragraph where a busy manager will miss them.", "**IT and Software Jobs**", "IT is the one sector where the Pakistani format bends towards the international one. One page is normal. The personal block shrinks to almost nothing.", "Lead with skills and projects and links to your work. A GitHub or portfolio link does more for you than a declaration paragraph ever will.", "**Government Jobs**", "Read the ad before you touch your CV. Many government jobs through PPSC and FPSC and NTS run on their own printed forms and your CV is only a supporting paper.", "Where a CV is asked for keep the full personal block with domicile and write education with board and division in the old formal way. Attach documents only in the order the ad demands and never add papers nobody asked for.", "**Gulf and Saudi Jobs**", "Employers in the Gulf read Pakistani CVs every single day so the base format feels familiar to them. A photo is commonly expected there. Your trade and your availability and your passport status matter more to them than your domicile does.", "Keep the CV to two pages and put your strongest trade skills near the top where a busy reader sees them first. If you can join immediately say so in one honest line. And for the full detail read our separate guide on the [CV for Gulf and Saudi jobs](https://cvmakerpakistan.com/cv-for-gulf-saudi-jobs) after this one."] },
 { h: "Length and Layout and the File You Send", p: ["Two pages is the standard length for most Pakistani jobs. Freshers stay on one page. Senior people with fifteen years of real work may use three and that is the ceiling.", "Use one clean font at a size your father could read without glasses. Calibri and Arial never embarrass anyone. Margins should breathe. A page filled edge to edge looks like a panic attack on paper. And if you ever feel tempted to shrink the font until everything somehow fits then stop right there and cut words instead because tiny font is a confession that the writer could not decide what actually matters.", "Bold your headings and your job titles and nothing else. When everything is bold nothing is bold.", "Save the final file as PDF unless the ad asks for Word. PDF keeps your layout fixed on every phone and every old office computer in the country.", "Name the file like a professional. Ahmed Khan CV.pdf tells the truth in three words. Final final new CV2.pdf tells a story you do not want told.", "And print it once before you send it. Half the layout disasters of this world are visible only on paper."] },
 { h: "Mistakes That Get Pakistani CVs Rejected", p: ["These are the mistakes I see most often when people show me their CVs for a check. Read them slowly. Most candidates make at least two of them.", "1. A phone number with one digit wrong. It sounds small. It ends the story before it starts.", "2. Education written without matric or without marks. Local recruiters read that as hiding something.", "3. One same CV sent to every job. A bank and a software house are not looking for the same human.", "4. A full CNIC number and home address printed for the whole internet to keep forever.", "5. Spelling that changes between the CV and the CNIC. One letter is enough to stall your joining file.", "6. Experience lines copied from a sample with numbers that are not yours. One interview question exposes them.", "7. A photo in sunglasses or at a wedding or inside a car. Yes. I have seen all three.", "8. A design so fancy that scanning software cannot read it at all. The machine quietly rejects what the eye enjoys.", "9. An email address made as a joke years ago in college. A silly nickname address does not belong on a serious job application.", "None of these need money to fix. They need one honest evening and a fresh pair of eyes and maybe a cup of chai that slowly goes cold on the table while you work."] },
 { h: "Will a Computer Reject Your CV Before a Human Sees It", p: ["And do not think for a moment that this is only a foreign problem. The big Pakistani companies in telecom and banking and fast moving consumer goods all run some version of this scan today and the smaller companies are following them fast. Many large companies in Pakistan now scan CVs with software before any human reads them. The software looks for standard headings and clean readable text and the exact words used in the job ad. Fancy designs confuse it badly. Tables and text boxes and skill bars drawn as pictures can make your best lines completely invisible to the machine.", "You do not need tricks to pass that scan. You need a simple layout and standard section names and your real skills written in the same words the ad uses for them.", "Before you apply to a large company run your CV through our free [ATS resume checker](https://cvmakerpakistan.com/ats-resume-checker) once. It shows you the missing sections and the weak spots in plain language. Fix what it flags honestly and then send the CV with a calm heart."] },
 { h: "A Five Minute Checklist Before You Apply", p: ["Before any application leaves your phone or laptop run through this list once. It takes five minutes and it saves careers. If you cannot print this list on paper then keep it printed inside your head because you will need it before every single application you send from today onwards.", "- My phone number and email are typed correctly and match on every page", "- My name and father name match my CNIC spelling exactly", "- Education shows degree and intermediate and matric with marks and board and year", "- Every experience bullet that can carry a number carries one", "- The CV is two pages or less and the file is a PDF with my name in the file name", "- The skills section matches the words used in this job ad", "- My email looks professional and the CV has passed one ATS check with no missing section", "If all seven boxes tick true your CV is already ahead of most of the pile it will land in.", "And if building all this in Word feels like a fight you can build the same format free in our [free online CV maker](https://cvmakerpakistan.com/resume-builder) and print it to PDF without any sign up. Your data stays in your browser. Nothing is uploaded anywhere and nothing is stored on a server."] },
 { h: "A Complete Sample CV: Copy the Shape Not the Words", p: ["Here is the full format filled in once so you can see it living on one page. The person below is fictional. Her marks and her numbers are invented for teaching. Copy the shape of this sample and put your own truth inside it.", "Ayesha Khan", "Accounts Assistant", "Lahore. Phone 0300 1234567. Email ayeshakhan.example@gmail.com", "**Profile**", "Accounts assistant with two years of office experience in a textile trading company. Strong in daily cash entries and invoice filing and month end closing support. Looking for an accounts role in a Lahore based company where accuracy matters more than speed.", "**Education**", "- BCom. University of the Punjab. 2022. 68% marks", "- ICom. BISE Lahore. 2018. 841 marks out of 1100 (76%)", "- Matric Commerce. BISE Lahore. 2016. 872 marks out of 1100 (79%)", "**Work Experience**", "Accounts Assistant. Star Textile Traders. Lahore. 2022 to present.", "- Recorded daily cash and bank entries for an office with monthly turnover of Rs 4500000", "- Prepared 300 plus sales invoices every month with a filing system that cut document search time from minutes to seconds", "- Helped close the monthly accounts on the third working day for eleven straight months", "**Skills**", "MS Excel with VLOOKUP and pivot tables. QuickBooks data entry. Invoice and voucher filing. Typing speed 38 words per minute.", "**Languages**", "Urdu. English. Punjabi.", "**References**", "Available on request.", "Read that sample once more with a cold eye. Every number on it can be questioned in an interview and answered without fear. That is the test your own CV must pass before it leaves your hands."] },
 { h: "Final Words Before You Hit Send", p: ["It is only two pages of paper or one small file sitting on a phone screen yet the weight it carries is honestly unfair because it walks into rooms you will never enter yourself and speaks on your behalf to people who will never hear your actual voice.", "Nothing about that boy from Faisalabad really changed in those two weeks if you think about it honestly. His degree stayed the same and his marks stayed the same and even the boy himself stayed the same. The only thing that changed was that a stranger could finally read his worth in under a minute without getting lost somewhere on the way. That in the end is all a good format quietly does for a person.", "Take one evening. Rebuild your CV in the order this guide gives you. Then send it with a calm heart and let it do its work. And when the interview call finally comes do not thank this format or thank me. Thank the evening you gave it and then walk into that room and be exactly the person your CV promised you are."] },
 { h: "A Short Note From the Writer", p: ["My name is Muhammad Kashif and I built the free CV maker on this site after watching good candidates around me lose interviews to bad formatting. Every rule in this guide comes from real CVs that people showed me for a check and from the mistakes I kept seeing again and again. I am not a recruiter. I am the person who fixes the document before the recruiter sees it. That is exactly the view this guide is written from and it is the only claim I will make here.", "First written for the 2026 hiring season and refreshed again this October after another round of checking real CVs that people brought to me."] }
], faq: [{ q: "What is the standard CV format in Pakistan?", a: "The standard format runs two pages in a fixed order. Contact details and a short objective come first. Education follows from the newest degree down to matric with marks and board. Then comes experience and skills and certifications. A personal block with father name and domicile is added for government and traditional employers. Follow this order and a recruiter can judge your whole case in seconds and that speed quietly works in your favour instead of against you." }, { q: "Should I include my photo in a Pakistani CV?", a: "It honestly depends on the sector you are applying in and a little bit on the city as well if we are being truthful. Government offices and sales teams and school jobs usually expect a plain professional photo sitting on the CV. Software houses and multinational companies usually prefer no photo at all and some of them even remove photos before the review begins. When the job ad says nothing at all follow the habit of that field and whatever you finally choose never send a selfie or a wedding crop no matter how good you honestly think you look in it." }, { q: "How do I write FSc and matric in my CV?", a: "This is the small place where most freshers quietly lose marks without even knowing it happened. Write the full group name first and then the board and year and your marks out of the total with the percentage. For example FSc Pre Engineering from BISE Lahore in 2019 with 912 marks out of 1100. That single line answers every question a local recruiter asks about that level of your education." }, { q: "Is one page or two pages better for a CV in Pakistan?", a: "For freshers one page is honestly enough because at this stage of life the story is still short and a single tight honest page always looks more confident than two pages padded with school hobbies and prize lists from years ago. Experienced candidates should use two pages and fill them with numbered results instead of duties. Going past three pages almost always means the CV carries old and irrelevant detail that should have been cut weeks ago." }, { q: "Can I use the same CV for a bank job and a Gulf job?", a: "No. The base order stays the same but the details bend with the reader. A bank wants formality and computer skills in clear lines. A Gulf employer wants your trade and your availability and often a photo. It honestly takes ten minutes at most to adjust the summary and the skills and to push your strongest points higher for each reader and those ten minutes are usually the difference between looking almost right for the job and looking made for that job." }] },
{ slug: "cv-for-freshers-pakistan", guide: true, title: "CV Format for Freshers in Pakistan With No Experience: First CV Guide With Sample", desc: "Learn the CV format for freshers in Pakistan with no experience with the right one page order and honest objective examples plus a full sample. Start free.", intro: "A girl from Gujranwala finished her BCom last year with marks that her whole street was proud of. For three straight months she applied everywhere and not one single call came back. We rebuilt her CV on one single page in one evening and within a month she had one internship offer and two interview calls for trainee posts.", sections: [
 { h: "Why Freshers Get Ignored Even With Good Marks", p: ["Quick Answer: A recruiter hiring for a trainee post already knows you have no experience. He is looking for signs of seriousness and clear writing and readiness to learn. Your CV has ten seconds to send those signs.", "Let me tell you the truth that nobody says out loud at a university farewell.", "A recruiter hiring for a trainee post already knows you have no experience. He is not looking for experience on your page. He is looking for signs. Signs that you are serious. Signs that you can write clearly. Signs that you finished things you started. Signs that you will show up on time and learn fast without making his life difficult.", "Your CV has about ten seconds to send those signs before his hand moves to the next file.", "A three page CV full of copied lines sends the opposite signs. It says this person did not think about my time. It says this person copies what others write. It says this person cannot decide what matters and what does not. None of that is true about you as a human being. But the paper says it and the paper is the only voice you have in that room.", "That is why format matters more for a fresher than for anyone else. An experienced man can survive a weak format because his jobs speak for him. You have no jobs yet. Your format is doing almost all of the speaking."] },
 { h: "The One Page Rule for First CV in Pakistan", p: ["Quick Answer: One page is correct for a fresher in Pakistan. Page two is almost never read. Page three is a joke. One tight page forces your best things to the top where eyes actually go.", "Every fresher asks me the same scared question. Is one page not too little for everything I have done?", "No. One page is honestly more than enough for a life that is twenty one or twenty two years old and most of it spent inside classrooms. One page is not little. One page is correct.", "Understand what extra pages really do to you. Page two of a fresher CV is almost never read. Page three is a joke that experienced staff sometimes pass around the office. I am not saying this to be cruel. I am saying it so you cut before someone else laughs.", "One tight page does something beautiful for you. It forces your best things to the top where eyes actually go. It makes you look decided and mature. And it respects the reader in a way that older candidates with ten years of work often fail to do.", "If your CV is running to two pages at this age it almost always means one of three things. You listed every subject you ever studied. You wrote paragraphs where lines belonged. Or you added sections that add nothing. All three are fixable in one sitting."] },
 { h: "Exact Order of a Fresher CV in Pakistan", p: ["Quick Answer: Name and post first and then a short objective and education from degree down to matric and projects and internships and skills matching the job and certifications and languages and references.", "A fresher CV follows its own order and it is not the same order as an experienced CV. Education moves up. Experience changes its name and its meaning. Here is the order that works.", "- Your full name and the title of the post you want", "- Phone number and email and city", "- A short objective of two or three honest lines written by you", "- Education from your newest degree down to matric", "- Projects and internships and any real work you have done", "- Skills that match the post you are applying for", "- Short courses and certifications if you honestly have them", "- Languages", "- One line saying references are available on request", "Notice what moved. Education sits near the top because right now it is your strongest proof. Projects and internships sit where experience would sit on an older man's CV because for you they are your experience and you should treat them with full respect.", "Keep this order and a recruiter can read your whole case in one smooth pass without stopping anywhere to hunt.", "Notice also what is missing. There is no declaration paragraph left over from your grandfather's time. There is no list of your father's occupation and your blood group. And there is no room anywhere for hobbies like watching cricket and listening to music unless the job itself is about cricket or music."] },
 { h: "Fresher CV Objective Examples That Don't Sound Copied", p: ["Quick Answer: Write your objective in plain words. Say what you studied and what post you want and one true strength you can prove on the same page. Three lines are enough.", "The objective is the first thing you write and the first place where freshers get caught copying.", "Open any five fresher CVs from the same university and you will find the same two lines sitting on all five like a uniform. Seeking a challenging position in a dynamic organisation where I can utilise my skills. Every recruiter in Pakistan has read that line ten thousand times. His eyes slide over it the way eyes slide over a wall poster. It earns you nothing and it quietly marks you as a copier before he reaches your name properly.", "Write your objective like a human being talking about himself in plain words. Say what you studied. Say what kind of work you are looking for. Say one true strength that you can prove later on the same page.", "A weak copied line says this. To work in a reputed organisation and enhance my abilities for the growth of the company.", "A strong honest line says this. BCom graduate from Gujranwala looking for a trainee accounts post. Strong in Excel and daily cash entries from a four month internship in a trading office. Ready to join immediately.", "Feel the difference in your own chest. The second one sounds like a person. The first one sounds like a photocopy of a photocopy.", "Three lines are enough. Never write an objective longer than the work it is introducing."] },
 { h: "Education Format for Fresh Graduates in Pakistan", p: ["Quick Answer: Write your degree first and then intermediate and then matric. Include board and year and marks at every level. Never skip a weak level because a missing level looks like hiding.", "Your education section is your degree and your ladder and it must be written the way Pakistani recruiters read it. Degree first. Then intermediate. Then matric. With board and year and marks written clearly at every level.", "Write every level in this shape.", "BCom. University of the Punjab. 2024. 68% marks", "ICom. BISE Gujranwala. 2020. 872 marks out of 1100 (79%)", "Matric Commerce. BISE Gujranwala. 2018. 901 marks out of 1100 (82%)", "For a degree where CGPA is the system write the CGPA honestly out of 4 and do not convert it into a percentage with your own secret formula. Recruiters know the real conversions and a padded number dies in the first interview question.", "Now the part where freshers panic. What if one level is weak? What if my FSc marks are low and my degree is strong?", "Write the weak level anyway. Every Pakistani recruiter expects the full ladder and a missing level does not look like a small accident to him. It looks like hiding. And hiding invites the worst guess his mind can make. A complete honest ladder with one weak step beats a broken ladder every single time.", "If you held any position or scholarship or distinction write it in one short line under that level. Third position in the board is gold on a fresher CV. Do not bury gold."] },
 { h: "No Job Yet? Your Experience Section Is Not Empty", p: ["Quick Answer: Internships and final year projects and freelance work and family business work and volunteer teaching all count as experience when you describe them with numbers and results.", "This is the section where freshers feel poor and I am here to tell you that the feeling is wrong.", "You have done real work in your life. You just have not been paid a salary for most of it yet and payment is not the only proof that work happened.", "Internships count fully. A final year project counts fully. Freelance work counts. Work in a family shop or family business counts when you describe it honestly. University society work and event management and volunteer teaching all count when they taught you something real and you can talk about them without fear.", "What turns these things into experience on paper is the same medicine as always. Numbers and results.", "Weak: Did internship in a bank", "Strong: Completed a three month internship at a bank branch in Gujranwala and processed account opening forms for 40 plus customers under supervision", "Weak: Made a final year project", "Strong: Built a final year project inventory system for a small pharmacy that cut stock counting time from two days to half a day", "Sit with your own life for ten quiet minutes and the material will come. The shop you helped in during summers. The cousin whose office files you arranged. The tuition you gave to the neighbour kids whose marks actually improved. Write it with numbers and it becomes experience. Leave it as one shy line and it stays invisible.", "One warning that can save your career before it starts. Never invent an internship or a job that never happened. Companies verify freshers more than anyone expects because they have been burned before. One phone call to that office ends the story."] },
 { h: "Skills for Fresher CV in Pakistan (Honest Only)", p: ["Quick Answer: Write 6 to 8 skills you can prove in a room. Job skills first and computer skills next and languages last. Skip copied words like leadership and strategic planning.", "Your skills section is small and powerful and it must be built only from things you can prove in a room.", "Write six to eight skills. Put the ones this job needs at the top. Split them in your head before you write. Job skills first. Computer skills next. Language skills last with honest levels.", "For office and accounts posts your honest weapons are things like Excel with real formulas and data entry speed and filing and basic accounting software if you truly used it during an internship. For IT posts your weapons are your languages and your tools and a link to your project work which does more talking than any line you can write. For teaching posts your weapons are your subjects and your classes and any demo lesson result you honestly earned.", "Two traps wait for you here. The first trap is the copied skills list where a quiet BCom graduate claims leadership and negotiation and strategic planning because a sample said so. The second trap is rating yourself with stars and bars that you invented at home. Both traps smell the same to a recruiter. They smell like decoration on an empty box.", "A line that says typing speed 35 words per minute and Excel formulas and pivot tables learned during internship will beat ten grand copied words every single day of the week."] },
 { h: "Complete Sample Fresher CV (Copy This Shape)", p: ["Quick Answer: Copy this shape and not the story. One page and an objective with real strength and a full education ladder with marks and an internship with numbers and honest skills.", "Here is the full CV format for freshers in Pakistan filled in once so you can see it living on one page. The sample below is an example built for a trainee accountant post and its marks and numbers are invented. Copy the shape of this sample and put your own truth inside it.", "**Your Full Name**", "Trainee Accountant", "Lahore. Phone 0300 0000000. Email yourname@example.com", "**Objective**", "BCom graduate looking for a trainee accountant post in Lahore. Strong in Excel and daily cash entries from a three month internship in a textile trading office. Ready to join immediately and ready to learn the full accounts cycle.", "**Education**", "BCom. University of the Punjab. 2024. 68% marks", "ICom. BISE Lahore. 2020. 845 marks out of 1100 (77%)", "Matric Commerce. BISE Lahore. 2018. 878 marks out of 1100 (80%)", "**Internship and Project Work**", "Accounts Intern. A textile trading office. Lahore. Three months in 2024.", "- Recorded daily cash entries for an office with monthly turnover of Rs 3800000 under the senior accountant", "- Filed and arranged more than 600 purchase invoices into a monthly system that the office still uses", "- Helped prepare the monthly salary sheet for 22 staff members twice without a single error", "Final Year Project. A simple stock register system for a small pharmacy in Lahore built with two classmates. The pharmacy shifted its stock counting from two days to half a day after using it for one full month.", "**Skills**", "MS Excel with VLOOKUP and pivot tables. Data entry at 35 words per minute. Invoice and voucher filing. Basic QuickBooks entries learned during internship.", "**Languages**", "Urdu. English. Punjabi.", "**References**", "Available on request.", "Read that sample once more with fresh eyes. Nothing on it is grand. Nothing on it is fake. And yet it would beat most three page fresher CVs in a real pile because every line on it can survive an interview question.", "Build your own one page CV with these same sections in our [free CV maker](https://cvmakerpakistan.com/resume-builder). No sign up and no upload and your data stays in your browser."] },
 { h: "Mistakes Freshers Make in First CV (Avoid These)", p: ["Quick Answer: A three page CV and a copied objective and every semester subject listed and a declaration paragraph and fake experience and family as reference and a selfie photo and one same CV for all jobs.", "I check fresher CVs more than any other kind and the same mistakes return like a season. Read them slowly and count how many are sitting on your CV right now.", "1. A three or four page CV at the age of twenty one. Length is not respect and nobody reads page three.", "2. An objective copied word for word from a website. Recruiters can smell a copied line before they finish it.", "3. Every subject of every semester listed like a result card. Your degree line already carries that story.", "4. A declaration paragraph from another century swearing that everything is true and correct. Nobody asked and nobody believes paper oaths.", "5. Experience that never happened. One verification call ends the story and sometimes ends it for the whole industry circle.", "6. A father or an uncle written as a reference. Family cannot judge your work and every recruiter knows it.", "7. A photo in a selfie angle or at a wedding or with sunglasses on your head. Yes. Fresher CVs have shown me all three.", "8. One same CV sent to a bank and a software house and a school. Three different readers need three different strongest points on top.", "9. Marks written without board or year or total. A line that cannot be checked cannot be trusted.", "Not one of these needs money to fix. They need one honest evening and the courage to cut what you lovingly added."] },
 { h: "Will ATS Read Your First CV Before a Human Does", p: ["Quick Answer: Big companies run ATS software that scans CVs before a human sees them. Use standard headings and clean text and the same words the job ad uses. Run your CV through a free ATS checker.", "For trainee posts in big companies the answer is often yes and freshers are usually the last people to find out.", "Banks and telecom companies and large groups run software that scans every CV and sorts the pile before a human being sees a single page. The software wants standard headings and clean text and the words used in the job ad. Tables and fancy designs and skill bars drawn as pictures can make your best lines invisible to it.", "You do not need tricks against a machine. You need the simple format in this guide and your real skills written in the same words the ad uses for them. Before you apply to any large company run your CV through our [free ATS resume checker](https://cvmakerpakistan.com/ats-resume-checker) once and fix what it flags honestly. It takes two minutes and it can quietly save an application you really wanted."] },
 { h: "5-Minute Checklist Before You Send Your First CV", p: ["Quick Answer: One page and a PDF file and correct contact details and an honest objective and a full education ladder and an internship with numbers and skills matching the job ad and every line able to survive an interview.", "Before any application leaves your phone or laptop run through this list once. It takes five minutes and it has saved more freshers than any paid template ever will.", "- My CV is one page and the file is a PDF with my own name in the file name", "- My phone number and email are typed correctly and my email sounds like a serious person", "- My objective is written by me and names the kind of post I actually want", "- Education shows degree and intermediate and matric with marks and board and year", "- My internship and project lines carry honest numbers wherever numbers exist", "- The skills section matches the words used in this job ad", "- Nothing on this page is copied or invented and I can defend every line in an interview", "If all seven boxes tick true your small one page CV is already ahead of most of the pile it will land in.", "And if setting all this in Word feels like a fight you can build the same format free in our [free online CV maker](https://cvmakerpakistan.com/resume-builder) and print it to PDF without any sign up. Your data stays in your browser. Nothing is uploaded anywhere and nothing is stored on a server. If you want the full format after your first job you can read the complete [CV format in Pakistan](https://cvmakerpakistan.com/cv-format-pakistan) guide on this site."] },
 { h: "Final Words for the First Time Job Hunter", p: ["Your first CV feels small because your working life is still small. That is not a shameful thing. That is just the truth of being twenty one.", "The girl from Gujranwala did not gain new marks in that one evening. She gained a page that finally told the truth about her in the right order. The calls were already owed to her. The format was the only wall standing in the way collecting them.", "Take one evening. Cut your CV to one honest page in the order this guide gives you. Then apply with a calm heart and keep applying because the first job is the hardest door and every door after it opens a little easier.", "And one small request before you go. If this guide helped you then share it with one friend who is hunting for a first job this year. It takes ten seconds and it helps this free guide reach the freshers who need it most."] },
 { h: "A Short Note From the Writer", p: ["My name is Muhammad Kashif and I built the free CV maker on this site after watching good fresh graduates around me lose interviews to bad formatting. Almost every rule in this guide comes from a real fresher CV that someone showed me with hope in their eyes and mistakes on the page. I am not a recruiter. I am the person who fixes the document before the recruiter sees it and this guide is written from exactly that chair."] },
], faq: [{ q: "How to write first CV in Pakistan with no experience?", a: "Quick Answer: Start with your name and the post you want and then a short objective with what you studied and then education from degree down to matric with marks and then internships and projects with numbers and then honest skills. Keep it one page." }, { q: "Is one page really enough for a fresher CV in Pakistan?", a: "Quick Answer: Yes. One page is correct for most freshers. A second page usually carries subjects and copied lines that weaken the case. Recruiters reading trainee files expect one page and respect it." }, { q: "What should a fresher write in the experience section?", a: "Quick Answer: Write your internships and final year projects and freelance work and family business work and volunteer work and describe all of them with numbers and results. A three month internship written with real duties and real figures reads as experience." }, { q: "Should a fresher add a photo to the CV?", a: "Quick Answer: It depends on the field. Government and sales and teaching posts often expect a plain professional photo. IT and multinational companies usually prefer no photo. Never send a selfie or a wedding crop." }, { q: "Should I write my matric marks if my degree marks are strong?", a: "Quick Answer: Yes. Pakistani recruiters read the full ladder from degree down to matric. A missing level looks like hiding even when your degree is shining. A complete honest ladder builds trust." }, { q: "Can I apply for jobs with no experience at all?", a: "Quick Answer: Yes. Trainee and junior posts exist exactly for freshers. Companies judge your education and projects and honesty and readiness to learn. They punish CVs that pretend to have experience." }] },
{ slug: "cv-for-gulf-saudi-jobs", guide: true, title: "CV for Gulf and Saudi Jobs: The Format Recruiters Read With a Sample", desc: "Learn the CV format for Gulf and Saudi jobs from Pakistan with the right order and what to remove plus a full sample. Build your Gulf CV free online today.", intro: "A young man from Rawalpindi spent eight months chasing a job in Saudi Arabia and heard nothing back from anyone. His CV carried his CNIC number and his father name and his full home address while his four years of real store work sat at the bottom in two thin lines. We rebuilt that CV on the Gulf format in one evening and within six weeks two Saudi companies called him for interviews.", sections: [
 { h: "Why Gulf Recruiters Read Your CV Differently", p: ["Quick answer. A Gulf recruiter reads your CV for three things in the first minute. Can this man do the exact job I am filling and is he ready to travel and join fast and will his paper survive the portal software that filters my pile. If your first page answers none of these you are out before a human ever saw your name.", "A Pakistani employer and a Gulf recruiter are not reading the same document even when the words are identical.", "The Pakistani reader knows your world. He knows your boards and your cities and your degree names and he reads your family details without blinking. The Gulf recruiter knows none of this. He is often sitting in Riyadh or Dubai or Doha with two hundred CVs for one storekeeper post and a software screen that already threw away half of them. He searches for the job title and the years and the systems and the keywords of his trade. Everything else is noise to him and noise gets closed.", "Notice what he never searches for. Your CNIC number means nothing to him and printing it in full is a risk to you and a waste to him. Your father name tells him nothing. Your full street address tells him nothing. The whole personal diary that opens a Pakistani CV is dead weight at a Gulf door.", "Write your CV so the three answers he came for jump off the first page. The job you fit. The proof you have done it. And the readiness to join. Do that and half your battle is won before any agent makes a single call."] },
 { h: "The Exact Order of a CV for Gulf and Saudi Jobs", p: ["Quick answer. Name and the exact job title you want first and then your contact lines with your country code and then a summary naming the job and your years and your readiness and then your experience with quantities and systems and then skills and languages and then your education and a short personal line with nationality and passport status.", "A CV for Gulf and Saudi jobs follows this order when it is built properly.", "1. Your full name", "2. The exact job title you are applying for", "3. Phone number with Pakistan country code and email and current city", "4. A short summary naming the job and your years and your Gulf readiness", "5. Work experience from the newest post down to the oldest with quantities", "6. Skills and systems and machines of your trade", "7. Languages with honest levels", "8. Education", "9. One short personal line. Nationality and date of birth and passport status and availability to travel", "10. References or one line saying references are available on request", "Look at where the weight sits. The job title sits in the second line because portal software and recruiter eyes both hunt for it there. The experience carries the page because Gulf hiring is experience hiring. And the personal details that fill half a Pakistani page shrink into one honest line at the bottom where a Gulf reader expects them.", "Pakistani government style posts and Pakistani company posts use a different order and our complete [CV format in Pakistan](https://cvmakerpakistan.com/cv-format-pakistan) guide covers that format on its own. This page is only for the Gulf door and the two should never be mixed."] },
 { h: "What to Remove From Your Pakistani CV Before You Send It", p: ["Quick answer. Remove your full CNIC number and your father name and your full home address and your religion and sect details before any Gulf application. They earn you nothing at a Gulf door and the CNIC number in full is a safety risk in any inbox.", "This section makes some readers uncomfortable so let me say it plainly. Nothing here is about hiding who you are. It is about sending the reader what he reads.", "Your CNIC number. A Gulf recruiter cannot use it and cannot verify it and does not ask for it at CV stage. Printed in full it only travels through agent inboxes and portal databases as a gift to anyone who misuses identity numbers. Take it off the Gulf version completely.", "Your father name. Pakistani forms ask for it because Pakistani systems are built that way. Gulf systems are not. Leave the line off the Gulf version.", "Your full home address. Your city and your country are enough for a recruiter to place you. Your street and house number help nobody hire you.", "Your religion and sect details. Gulf CV practice does not carry them and they do not change a recruiter shortlist. Leave them off the page.", "Two personal facts are different at this door and they stay. Your nationality stays because work permits are nationality based. And your date of birth usually stays inside the personal line because Gulf visas work with age limits and recruiters honestly check your age against the post. Marital status sits in between. Many agents and employers ask it on their own forms later so give it there when a form asks and keep your CV line short.", "What stays and grows instead is your trade. Your years. Your quantities. Your systems. Your languages. That is the exchange this format makes and it is why the Rawalpindi storekeeper started getting calls on the same four years that had been silent for eight months."] },
 { h: "Your Summary Must Name the Job and the Gulf Readiness", p: ["Quick answer. Your summary must name the exact job title and your years and your strongest trade proof and your readiness to travel in the first three lines. A copied line about seeking a bright future in a growing organisation earns you nothing at this door.", "Most Gulf summaries fail in the first line because they were written for no job and no country at all.", "A weak summary says this. A hardworking and honest person seeking a bright future in a growing organisation where I can use my abilities for mutual growth and success.", "Read that line again and tell me one thing you learned. Nothing. No job. No years. No proof. It could sit on five hundred applications in the same portal pile and that is exactly why it earns nothing.", "A strong summary says this. Storekeeper with four years of warehouse experience in Rawalpindi. Handled stock of 3000 plus items with daily receiving and dispatch records in Excel based registers. Ready to travel and join within one month. Valid Pakistani passport in hand.", "Every word of that second summary is doing work. Job named. Years named. Scale named. Readiness named. Passport named. A recruiter can decide in ten seconds that this file deserves his next ten minutes.", "Write your summary in that shape even if your years are fewer. One honest trade with real numbers beats grand empty words every day of the week. And if this is your very first job hunt with no experience at all then build the page on the fresher shape first with our [CV format for freshers in Pakistan](https://cvmakerpakistan.com/cv-for-freshers-pakistan) guide and then bend it with the Gulf order in this guide."] },
 { h: "Experience Written the Way Gulf Recruiters Search", p: ["Quick answer. Write every post with the job title the Gulf market uses and the company type and the town and your quantities and your systems. Recruiters and their software search for titles and trade words and the man whose words match the search is the man who gets the call.", "This is the heart of your Gulf CV and it is where most Pakistani applicants silently lose the post before any human reads a line.", "A duty line tells the reader you stood in a warehouse or on a site or behind a counter. A searched line tells him you did the exact work he is hiring for with the exact words his software is hunting.", "Weak: Worked as a storekeeper and handled inventory", "Strong: Storekeeper in a building material warehouse in Rawalpindi. Received and issued stock of 3000 plus items. Kept daily stock registers in MS Excel and ran the monthly stock count with zero shortage reported in my last year", "Weak: Worked as a salesman in a shop", "Strong: Salesman in a mobile phone retail shop in Lahore. Sold 40 plus handsets a month and handled cash closing and stock display for two full years", "Every strong line carries the same gifts. The market title of the job. The type of business. The town. And a quantity or a system that a reference call could confirm.", "Use the job titles the Gulf market itself uses on its portals because those are the words in the search box. Storekeeper and warehouse assistant and light vehicle driver and steel fixer and shuttering carpenter and AC technician and salesman and waiter and security guard and data entry operator. If your Pakistani title says something grander and vaguer then translate it honestly into the market title that matches the work you truly did. Honest translation is not lying. It is speaking the reader language.", "And one warning from my heart. Never buy fake Gulf experience from an agent and never write a Saudi or Dubai post you never held. Gulf employers verify through calls and through the small world of their own trade and one invented post can close every Gulf door to you for years. Pakistan experience written strongly beats fake Gulf experience every single time.", "Before your CV goes onto any portal run it once through our [free ATS resume checker](https://cvmakerpakistan.com/ats-resume-checker) so you know the machine reads your titles and your quantities the way you wrote them. Most Gulf applications pass through software before they ever reach a human hand."] },
 { h: "Saudi Arabia Specifics That Recruiters Check", p: ["Quick answer. For Saudi posts keep your passport line honest and clear and if you are already inside Saudi Arabia with a transferable status then that fact gets its own clear line because it changes how fast a company can take you.", "Saudi Arabia takes the largest share of Pakistani workers in the Gulf and its recruiters read with a few specific habits worth knowing.", "They ask about your passport early. Keep the passport line on your CV simple and true. Valid passport in hand or passport applied for. Never write a passport number on a CV that travels through portals and agent inboxes. The number belongs to the visa stage and to your own safety.", "They care about readiness and notice period more than Pakistani recruiters do because their projects and their shops staff on dates. One honest line about how soon you can travel and join earns real weight. Ready to join immediately or ready within one month. Write only what is true for your house and your current job.", "If you have already worked in Saudi Arabia then your past Saudi experience moves up the page even above newer Pakistan work because a man who knows the country and the work rhythm there is a lower risk in their eyes. And if you are applying from inside the Kingdom with a status your sponsor allows to transfer then say so in one plain line. Recruiters ask this question out loud in the first call so the CV should answer it before the call.", "Trade certificates and driving licences follow the same rule. If you hold a Saudi or Pakistani driving licence and the post involves driving then it gets a line of its own. If you hold a trade test certificate then name it. Papers that change a hiring decision should never hide inside a paragraph."] },
 { h: "Tests and Papers That Come After the CV", p: ["Quick answer. The CV wins you the selection and then the real Gulf paperwork begins. Trade tests and a medical and a police certificate all live after the selection and none of them belong on the CV itself except one honest line for a test you have already passed.", "Workers lose money and months by mixing these two stages together so let me separate them with a hard line.", "For many Saudi trade posts there is now a skill verification test under the Takamol programme where a man proves his trade with his hands before the visa moves forward. Electricians and plumbers and welders and mechanics meet this stage most often. If you have already passed such a test then the certificate earns one clear line on your CV because it answers the biggest doubt of the recruiter in advance. If you have not passed it yet then write nothing and let your experience lines speak.", "Engineers heading to Saudi Arabia meet the Saudi Council of Engineers because engineering job titles there are registered titles and companies ask about that registration early. Health workers meet their own licensing road where exams such as Prometric sit on the employer side of the process. Drivers should know that a licence story matters in this order. Your Pakistani licence proves you can drive and a Saudi or GCC licence from earlier Gulf work is worth its own proud line because companies value a man who can start driving legally from the first week.", "After selection the visa stage asks for a medical test through the approved Gulf medical programme that most workers still call GAMCA and for a police clearance certificate from your own district. These papers belong to the visa file and never to the CV. Any agent who asks you to print your medical history or your family record on the CV itself is teaching you the wrong document."] },
 { h: "The Other Gulf Doors. UAE and Qatar and Oman", p: ["Quick answer. The same Gulf format works for the Emirates and Qatar and Oman and Bahrain and Kuwait with only small bends. The job title and the quantities and the readiness lines matter at every Gulf door and the personal diary matters at none of them.", "Workers sometimes ask me if Dubai wants a different CV from Riyadh. The honest answer is that the bones are the same and only the emphasis moves a little.", "The Emirates hire heavily into sales and hospitality and logistics and construction and their portals are crowded beyond imagination. There your market job title and your English level carry extra weight because customer facing work dominates. Write your English honestly and let your sales numbers speak.", "Qatar hires in waves around projects and facilities and its recruiters lean on agencies and on trade words. Your trade title and your certificates do the heavy lifting there.", "Oman and Bahrain and Kuwait take smaller numbers of Pakistani workers and rely even more on forwarded CVs through people already inside. There a clean one or two page CV that a cousin or a friend can forward on a phone without shame is worth more than a decorated five page file. Every Gulf version of your CV should pass the phone test. It should read clean on a small screen in bright light because that is honestly how half of them get read.", "One base CV in this format bent slightly for the country you are applying to will always beat one Pakistani CV thrown at all six doors."] },
 { h: "A Complete Sample CV for Gulf Jobs. Copy the Shape. Not the Story.", p: ["Quick answer. Copy the shape of this sample and not the story. The market job title under the name and the quantities inside the experience lines and one honest personal line at the bottom and your own truth inside the same shape.", "Here is the full Gulf format filled in once so you can see it living on a page. The sample below is an example built for a storekeeper post and its posts and numbers are invented. Copy the shape of this sample and put your own truth inside it.", "**Your Full Name**", "Storekeeper", "Rawalpindi. Phone with country code +92 300 0000000. Email yourname@example.com", "**Summary**", "Storekeeper with four years of warehouse experience in Rawalpindi. Handled stock of 3000 plus items with daily receiving and dispatch records in Excel based registers. Ready to travel and join within one month. Valid Pakistani passport in hand.", "**Experience**", "Storekeeper. A building material warehouse. Rawalpindi. 2022 to present.", "- Received and issued stock of 3000 plus items including cement and steel and sanitary material", "- Kept daily stock registers in MS Excel and ran the monthly stock count with zero shortage reported in the last year", "- Checked supplier deliveries against purchase orders and reported damage and shortage on the same day", "Warehouse Assistant. A general trading store. Rawalpindi. 2020 to 2022.", "- Packed and loaded dispatch orders for city and outstation customers", "- Arranged shelves and FIFO stock rotation for dated material", "**Skills**", "Stock keeping and inventory counts. MS Excel registers. Receiving and dispatch documentation. Loading supervision. Forklift assistance experience.", "**Languages**", "Urdu. English working level. Arabic basic spoken words for warehouse work.", "**Education**", "- Intermediate. BISE Rawalpindi. 2019", "- Matric. BISE Rawalpindi. 2017", "**Personal Line**", "Nationality Pakistani. Date of birth 14 August 1998. Valid passport in hand. Ready to travel and join within one month.", "**References**", "Available on request.", "Read that sample once more like a recruiter in Riyadh would. Job title in the second line. Quantities where the claims sit. One personal line instead of a personal diary. Nothing grand and nothing fake and yet it would survive any question on any call.", "Build your own Gulf CV with these same sections in our [free CV maker](https://cvmakerpakistan.com/resume-builder). No sign up and no upload and your data stays in your browser."] },
 { h: "Short Sample Lines for Other Trades", p: ["Quick answer. Every trade bends the same Gulf shape around its own work. A driver leads with his licence and his routes. A technician leads with his systems and his repairs. A salesman leads with his numbers and his market.", "One sample cannot carry every trade so here are three short shapes in the same format. Copy the shape of your own trade and put your own truth inside it.", "**Light Vehicle Driver**", "Driver with six years of experience in Lahore including three years of delivery and staff transport work. Valid Pakistani driving licence for car and jeep. Clean record with no major accident in six years. Ready to travel and available for shift duties.", "**AC Technician**", "AC technician with five years of installation and repair work in Multan. Installed and serviced split units of all major brands and handled complaint calls for a dealer with 40 plus regular building clients. Trade certificate from a government technical institute. Ready to travel and join within one month.", "**Salesman**", "Salesman with four years of FMCG distribution work in Faisalabad. Covered a route of 120 plus shops with weekly order booking and payment collection. Beat the monthly target in nine months of the last year. Valid Pakistani driving licence. Ready to travel and join immediately."] },
 { h: "Mistakes That Keep Good Workers Stuck at Home", p: ["Quick answer. The mistakes that keep good workers at home are a Pakistani personal block on a Gulf CV and duty lines instead of quantities and vague job titles no portal can find and fake Gulf experience bought from an agent and a passport number printed for anyone to steal.", "I read Gulf CVs in every hiring season and the same wounds return on page after page. Count how many of them are sitting on your CV right now.", "1. The full Pakistani personal block at the top. CNIC and father name and full address eating the space where your trade should stand.", "2. Experience written as duties only. Worked as storekeeper for four years tells a recruiter nothing about your stock or your systems or your counts.", "3. A job title nobody searches for. Grand vague titles hide you from the portal software that builds the shortlist.", "4. Fake Gulf experience written by an agent. One verification call ends it and the ending follows you for years.", "5. Your passport number printed on the CV. That number belongs to the visa stage and printing it early is a gift to identity thieves.", "6. No readiness line anywhere. The recruiter cannot tell if you can join in two weeks or in six months so he picks the man whose page answers him.", "7. A decorated CV with colours and tables and a wedding style photo. Gulf recruiters read on phones and through software and decoration breaks both.", "8. The same unbent CV sent to Saudi and Dubai and Qatar and a Pakistani company in the same week. Every door wants the same truth in a slightly different order.", "None of these need money or a paid service to fix. They need one honest evening and the format in this guide."] },
 { h: "A Five Minute Checklist Before You Send a Gulf Application", p: ["Quick answer. Seven boxes. Market job title under your name. No CNIC and no father name on the page. Quantities inside the experience lines. Languages with honest levels. One personal line with nationality and passport status. Every line able to survive a verification call.", "Before any application leaves your phone or laptop run through this list once. Five minutes of checking can protect eight months of silence.", "- My exact market job title sits under my name and my phone carries the Pakistan country code", "- The Gulf version of my CV carries no CNIC number and no father name and no full home address", "- My experience lines show quantities and systems and results that a reference call can confirm", "- My summary names my years and my readiness to travel and join", "- My languages are written with honest levels including any Arabic I truly hold", "- My personal line is one line only. Nationality and date of birth and passport status and availability", "- Nothing on this page is copied or invented and no agent has written a word of it for me", "If all seven boxes tick true your CV is already ahead of most of the pile on the recruiter screen.", "And if building all this in Word feels like a fight you can build the same format free in our [free online CV maker](https://cvmakerpakistan.com/resume-builder) and print it to PDF without any sign up. Your data stays in your browser. Nothing is uploaded anywhere and nothing is stored on a server."] },
 { h: "Final Words for the Workers Reading This", p: ["The Gulf has fed more Pakistani homes than any factory or office in this country and the door to it opens on a single page more often than people believe.", "The storekeeper from Rawalpindi did not become a better storekeeper in that one evening we spent on his CV. He became a readable one. His four years were always there. They were just written for the wrong reader in the wrong language of format.", "Take one evening before your next application round. Rebuild your CV for the reader who will actually hold it. Then send it with a calm heart and let your real work speak in the order this guide gives you.", "And one small request before you go. If this guide helped you then share it with one friend or cousin who is trying for the Gulf this year. It takes ten seconds and it helps this free guide reach the workers who need it most."] },
 { h: "A Short Note From the Writer", p: ["My name is Muhammad Kashif and I built the free CV maker on this site after watching good candidates around me lose interviews to bad formatting. Gulf CVs reach me carrying CNIC numbers and father names and agent written lies so often that I have stopped counting. Almost every rule in this guide comes from one of those real pages and the quiet mistakes sitting on it. I am not a recruiter and I am not an agent and I charge nothing for this guide. I am the person who fixes the document before the recruiter sees it and this guide is written from exactly that chair."] },
], faq: [{ q: "What should a CV for Saudi Arabia jobs from Pakistan include?", a: "Your name and the exact market job title and your phone with the Pakistan country code and a summary with your years and your readiness and then your experience with quantities and systems and then skills and languages and education and one personal line with your nationality and your date of birth and passport status. Leave the CNIC number and father name and full address off the Gulf version completely." }, { q: "Should I put a photo on my CV for Gulf jobs?", a: "For many Gulf posts a plain professional photo is accepted and sometimes expected at the agent and application stage while portal uploads often work without one. If you add one then use a simple head and shoulders photo with a clean background and formal dress. Never a selfie and never a wedding or function picture whatever the post." }, { q: "Is Arabic necessary for Gulf jobs?", a: "For most trade and warehouse and site posts basic spoken Arabic words help your daily work and honest basic level written on the CV is respected. For office and sales posts English carries more weight. Write only the level you truly hold because the first working week will test whatever the page claims." }, { q: "My agent says he will write my CV and add Gulf experience. Should I let him?", a: "No. An agent written CV with experience you never held is the most dangerous document in Gulf hiring because employers verify through calls and trade networks and one exposed invention can close Gulf doors to you for years. Write your real Pakistan experience strongly in the Gulf format instead. Honest pages travel further than decorated lies." }, { q: "How long should a CV for Gulf jobs be?", a: "One to two pages serve most workers best because recruiters read on phones and through portal software and short clean pages survive both. Workers with long multi country experience may honestly reach three pages. Anything longer usually means personal details and duty lines are sitting where your quantities and results should be shining." }] },
{ slug: "europe-cv-format", guide: true, title: "Europe CV Format: Europass, Germany, UK, Italy, Spain & Netherlands", desc: "Europe CV formats explained: Europass structure, German formal CVs, UK 2-page CVs, and the Dutch direct style.", intro: "Europe has no single CV format. Europass is the shared standard, but Germany, the UK, Italy, Spain and the Netherlands each have their own expectations. This guide covers the differences that matter.", sections: [
 { h: "Europass: the shared European format", p: ["Europass is a standard European CV format with fixed sections: personal information, work experience, education and training, personal skills and additional information. It is widely understood across the EU.", "Europass is safe and structured, but many recruiters prefer a tailored CV with the same information in a cleaner layout. Either way, keep dates consistent and the structure single-column."] },
 { h: "Germany and Italy", p: ["German CVs are formal and chronological, usually with a photo, date of birth, nationality and a full education timeline. Italian CVs are also Europass-compatible, with strong emphasis on degrees and language proficiencies.", "In both countries, list degrees with full names, institutions and dates, and languages with honest levels."] },
 { h: "UK and Netherlands", p: ["A UK CV runs up to 2 pages with a personal statement, detailed employment history and education. No photo is expected.", "Dutch CVs are direct: a 2-line profile, achievements with numbers, and plain language. A photo is not required in either country."] },
 { h: "Spain and Sweden", p: ["Spanish CVs are chronological with a professional objective and a skills matrix. Swedish CVs are minimalist, with a concise summary, teamwork evidence and verifiable experience.", "Across Europe, submit a text-based PDF unless the employer asks for another format. Build it with the free builder, then run the ATS resume checker."] }],
 faq: [{ q: "What is Europass?", a: "Europass is the standard Europass CV format with fixed sections. It is widely understood, but many recruiters prefer a tailored CV with the same information." }, { q: "Does a Europe CV need a photo?", a: "It depends on the country. Photos are common in Germany and Italy, and not expected in the UK, Netherlands or Sweden. Check the job posting. Note: this builder does not add photos — if one is required, add it in your document tool before printing." }, { q: "How long should a Europe CV be?", a: "Usually 2 pages. Keep it structured, factual and single-column so ATS software can read it." }] },
{ slug: "teacher-cv-format-pakistan", guide: true, title: "Teacher CV Format in Pakistan: Complete Guide With BEd & MEd Sample", desc: "Learn the teacher CV format in Pakistan with the right section order and a full sample CV. Write a school teacher CV that shows subjects and board results.", intro: "First written for the 2026 hiring season and refreshed again this October after another round of checking real teacher CVs that people brought to me. A chemistry teacher from Multan had taught for six years in a small private school near her home. Her students loved her and her board results were the best in her school two years running and yet the bigger schools she applied to stayed silent.", sections: [
 { h: "Why a Teacher CV Is Read Differently", p: ["Quick answer. A principal asks three questions in the first ten seconds. Which subjects can you teach and which classes can you handle and what did your students actually score. If your first page does not answer all three you are in the rejected pile.", "A bank manager reading a CV asks what systems this person can run. A principal reading a CV asks three much simpler questions and he asks them in this order.", "Which subjects can this person teach? Which classes can this person handle? And what did the students of this person actually score?", "That is the whole game. If your CV answers those three questions in the first ten seconds you are in the interview pile. If the principal has to dig through paragraphs of general claims to find your subjects you are in the other pile and the other pile is where good teachers quietly disappear.", "Notice what is not in his first three questions. He does not ask for your life story. He does not ask for a paragraph about your passion for education copied from the internet. Passion is proven in a classroom and on a results sheet. On paper it is proven by subjects and classes and numbers.", "Write your CV so those three answers jump off the page and half your battle is won before the interview is even announced."] },
 { h: "The Exact Order of a Teacher CV in Pakistan", p: ["Quick answer. Name and post first and then a summary with subjects and classes and then education with your BEd and then experience with board results and then training and skills and languages and references.", "A teacher CV in Pakistan follows this order when it is built properly.", "1. Your full name and the teaching post you want", "2. Phone number and email and city", "3. A short summary naming your subjects and your classes", "4. Education with your teaching qualification written clearly", "5. Teaching experience from the newest post down to the oldest", "6. Training and workshops and certifications", "7. Skills that belong to a classroom", "8. Languages", "9. References or one line saying references are available on request", "Look at where the weight sits. Your summary carries your subjects in its first breath because that is what the reader came for. Your education carries your BEd or MEd in full view because schools check it before they check almost anything else. And your experience is written as results and not as duties because teaching is one of the few jobs where your results are printed by a board for the whole world to see.", "Government educator posts bend this order a little and I will come to them in their own section below. Private schools and colleges follow the order above almost exactly."] },
 { h: "Your Summary Must Name Subjects and Classes", p: ["Quick answer. Your summary must name your subject and your classes and your city and one real result inside its first two lines. A copied line about seeking a challenging position earns you nothing at all.", "Most teacher summaries fail in the first line because they were written for no subject and no class at all.", "A weak summary says this. A dedicated and passionate teacher seeking a challenging position in a reputed institution to utilise my skills for the growth of students.", "Read that line again and tell me one thing you learned. Nothing. No subject. No class. No proof. It could sit on a bank clerk application without changing a single word and that is exactly why it earns nothing.", "A strong summary says this. MSc Chemistry teacher with six years of experience teaching classes nine to twelve in Multan. Board pass rate of my matric classes stayed above 90 percent for three straight years. BEd qualified and ready to join from the new session.", "Every word of that second summary is doing work. Subject named. Classes named. City named. Result named. Qualification named. A principal can decide in five seconds that this file deserves his next five minutes.", "Write your summary in that shape even if your numbers are smaller. Small true numbers beat grand empty words every day of the week."] },
 { h: "Education Written for Teaching Posts", p: ["Quick answer. Write your BEd or MEd with university and year and marks just like your degree and never hide it at the bottom of the page because government merit counts those marks with real weight.", "Schools read your education in two layers and your CV must serve both layers honestly.", "The first layer is your subject degree. MSc or MA or BS in your teaching subject with university and year and marks or CGPA written clean. If you teach two subjects because your degree and your experience honestly cover two then name both and be ready to defend both in a demo lesson.", "The second layer is your teaching qualification and this is where teacher CVs quietly lose posts. Your BEd or MEd is not a small extra line to hide at the bottom of the page. For most school posts it is a formal requirement and for government educator posts it carries actual marks weight in the merit. Write it with university and year and marks like any other degree.", "Write every level in this shape.", "- MSc Chemistry. University of the Punjab. 2018. 72% marks", "- BEd. Allama Iqbal Open University. 2020. 68% marks", "- FSc Pre Medical. BISE Multan. 2012. 889 marks out of 1100 (81%)", "- Matric Science. BISE Multan. 2010. 912 marks out of 1100 (83%)", "Yes. Matric and FSc belong on a teacher CV in Pakistan even when your MSc is strong. School managements and education departments both read the full ladder and government merit formulas literally count your earlier marks. A missing level on a teacher CV does not look modern. It looks careless.", "If your BEd is still in progress write it as in progress with the expected year. Schools understand an honest running degree. What they never forgive is finding a completed claim in the interview that your papers cannot support.", "**The BEd Teacher CV Format in Pakistan**", "A BEd teacher CV format in Pakistan stands or falls on one line and that line is the BEd entry itself. Write the university and the passing year and the marks in the same clean shape as your degree because school managements read that line before they read your experience. If your BEd marks are strong then that one line quietly argues for you in every merit list and every shortlist in the country."] },
 { h: "Teaching Experience That Shows Results", p: ["Quick answer. Replace your duty lines with result lines that carry your subject and your classes and one number that a board record or a school record can confirm.", "This is the heart of your CV and it is where most teachers undersell six years of work in two lines.", "A duty line tells the reader you stood in a classroom. A result line tells him what happened to the students standing in front of you. Principals hire results.", "- Weak: Worked as a science teacher in a private school", "- Strong: Taught chemistry to 140 students of classes nine and ten and raised the matric board pass rate of my sections from 78 percent to 93 percent in two years", "- Weak: Taught English to middle classes", "- Strong: Taught English to classes six to eight and prepared 60 students for the annual speech competition where three of my students took district level positions", "Every strong line carries the same three gifts. The subject. The classes. And a number that a board or a school record can confirm. That is the whole idea behind a teacher CV format with board results and it is the single change that moves the most teacher CVs out of the rejected pile and into the interview pile.", "Fresh teachers worry here and the worry is fair. If you are new then your teaching practice from your BEd and your internships and your demo lessons and your home tuition students are your experience and you should write them in this same shape with whatever honest numbers they carry. A tuition teacher whose five matric students all passed with above 80 percent has a real result line and it belongs on her CV without any shame.", "And one warning from my heart. Never invent board results. Education is a small world in every city of Pakistan and principals know each other and boards publish results and one invented percentage can follow a teacher for her whole career.", "And before your CV goes to any large school chain run it once through our [free ATS resume checker](https://cvmakerpakistan.com/ats-resume-checker) so you know the machine read your subjects and your results the way you wrote them."] },
 { h: "Private School and Government Teacher CV Format in Pakistan", p: ["Private schools want your subjects and your results on a clean CV of one to two pages. Government teacher posts want every mark from matric upward written clearly because your merit is counted in numbers. The base format is the same for both doors and only the weight shifts from one part to another.", "**Private School Teacher CV Format**", "Anyone searching for a teacher CV format for private school in Pakistan is really asking one thing and that is what a principal wants to see in the first ten seconds of reading. Private schools and school chains care about your demo lesson and your classroom energy and your results in their own exam system. They like a clean two page CV with your subjects on top and your experience written as results. A professional photo is commonly expected in this world. English medium chains also quietly test your spoken English from the first phone call so write only the English level you truly carry.", "**Government Teacher CV Format Pakistan**", "Government educator posts through the education department and testing services run on merit formulas and printed forms. Your CV there is a supporting paper and your marks at every level from matric to your master degree literally become merit numbers. Keep the full personal block with domicile for these posts and attach your documents exactly in the order the advertisement demands. Your BEd marks matter here in a way they never will in a private school interview.", "**College Lecturer Posts**", "College lecturer posts are a third door. There your CV may run longer because your subject degree and any research and any publications honestly belong on it. Keep the school style results for your teaching and add your academic record with a clean head.", "One base CV bent honestly for each door will always beat one lazy CV thrown at all three."] },
 { h: "Private School vs Government vs College: The Quick Difference", p: ["Length. Private school one to two pages. Government the printed form with your CV as a supporting paper. College two to four pages.", "Photo. Private school expected. Government required with the file. College optional.", "BEd weight. Private school important. Government counted in the merit formula. College important.", "Board results. Private school demo lesson plus board numbers. Government board numbers only. College board numbers plus research.", "Domicile. Private school not needed. Government required. College not needed.", "Documents. Private school not attached. Government attached in the order the advertisement demands. College optional.", "Private school posts are decided by your demo lesson and your board results together. Your CV runs one to two pages. A photo is commonly expected. Your BEd is required or strongly preferred and your spoken English matters from the first phone call.", "Government educator posts are decided by a merit formula before any human judges you. The printed form is the real application and your CV travels with it as a supporting paper. Every mark from matric to your master degree and your BEd counts in that formula and your domicile and photo are part of the file.", "College lecturer posts are decided by your subject degree and your academic record. Your CV may run longer because research and publications honestly belong on it. A photo is optional here and your teaching results still matter but they share the stage with your academic work."] },
 { h: "Skills That Belong to a Classroom", p: ["Quick answer. Write the skills a principal actually worries about and skip the copied corporate words that no classroom has ever seen.", "Your skills section should sound like a school and not like a software company.", "Write the skills a principal actually worries about. The subjects you can teach beyond your main subject if that claim is true. The classes you can control. Lesson planning. Paper setting and paper checking. Board exam preparation. Basic computer use for result sheets and smart class tools if you honestly use them. Parent meeting handling if you have truly done it.", "Split them in your head before you write. Teaching skills first. Computer skills next. Language skills last with honest levels in plain words.", "Leave out the grand copied words that no classroom has ever seen. Nobody has ever hired a class six teacher for her strategic leadership vision. They hire her because she can teach fractions to forty children at eight in the morning and keep every one of them awake. Write skills that sound like that morning."] },
 { h: "A Complete Sample Teacher CV. Copy the Shape. Not the Story.", p: ["Quick answer. Copy the shape of this sample and not the story. Subjects in the first line and BEd written clearly and board results with honest numbers and your own truth inside the same shape.", "Here is the full teacher format filled in once so you can see it living on a page. The sample below is an example built for teaching and its marks and numbers are invented. Copy the shape of this sample and put your own truth inside it.", "**Your Full Name**", "Chemistry Teacher", "Multan. Phone 0300 0000000. Email yourname@example.com", "**Summary**", "MSc Chemistry teacher with six years of experience teaching classes nine to twelve in Multan. Matric board pass rate of my sections stayed above 90 percent for three straight years. BEd qualified and available to join from the new academic session.", "**Education**", "- MSc Chemistry. University of the Punjab. 2018. 72% marks", "- BEd. Allama Iqbal Open University. 2020. 68% marks", "- FSc Pre Medical. BISE Multan. 2012. 889 marks out of 1100 (81%)", "- Matric Science. BISE Multan. 2010. 912 marks out of 1100 (83%)", "**Teaching Experience**", "Chemistry Teacher. A private girls high school. Multan. 2020 to present.", "- Taught chemistry to 140 students of classes nine and ten across three sections", "- Raised the matric board pass rate of my sections from 78 percent to 93 percent in two years", "- Prepared the annual chemistry practical files for board inspection for four straight sessions", "Science Teacher. A model school. Multan. 2018 to 2020.", "- Taught general science to classes six to eight with an average class size of 38 students", "- Ran the school science exhibition team that took second position at district level in 2019", "**Training and Workshops**", "- Two day workshop on board paper pattern and marking scheme. BISE Multan. 2023", "- Online course on classroom assessment basics. 2022", "**Skills**", "Chemistry and general science teaching. Lesson planning and paper setting. Board exam preparation. Result sheet preparation in MS Excel. Parent teacher meeting handling.", "**Languages**", "Urdu. English. Saraiki.", "**References**", "Available on request.", "Read that sample once more like a principal would. Subjects in the first line. Results in the second minute. Nothing grand and nothing fake and yet it would survive any interview question asked anywhere in Pakistan."] },
 { h: "How Long Should a Teacher CV Be in Pakistan", p: ["One to two pages for school posts. College lecturer posts can run longer when you honestly have research or publications to show.", "That is the short answer and it surprises teachers who arrive with five pages of attachments and course lists. A principal reads your subjects and your results in the first minute and decides your fate there. Everything after page two is almost never the reason a teacher gets called. Keep the CV tight and let the documents travel separately when a post actually asks for them."] },
 { h: "Mistakes That Keep Good Teachers Unemployed", p: ["Quick answer. The mistakes that keep good teachers unemployed are missing subjects in the summary and missing board results on the page and a buried BEd and experience written as duties instead of results.", "I read teacher CVs every hiring season and the same wounds return on page after page. Count how many of them are sitting on your CV right now.", "1. No subjects or classes named in the summary. The principal came for those two answers and the CV made him dig for them.", "2. Board results nowhere on the page. In teaching your results are published proof and hiding them looks like having none.", "3. BEd or MEd missing or buried at the bottom. For many posts it is a formal requirement and for government merit it carries real weight.", "4. Experience written as duties only. Worked as a teacher for six years tells a principal almost nothing about your classroom.", "5. A claim to teach all subjects at all levels. Nobody believes it and it quietly cancels the subjects you truly can teach.", "6. The same CV sent to a school post and a college lecturer post and a government educator post. Three doors need three honest versions.", "7. A wedding photo or a selfie on the CV. Schools are conservative rooms and the photo speaks before you do.", "8. Invented results or invented training. Education circles in every city are small and one phone call between principals ends the story.", "None of these need money or a paid service to fix. They need one honest evening and the format in this guide."] },
 { h: "A Five Minute Checklist Before You Apply for a Teaching Post", p: ["Before any application leaves your phone or laptop run through this list once. Five minutes of checking can protect six years of teaching.", "- My summary names my subjects and my classes in its very first lines", "- My board results or class results appear with honest numbers in my experience", "- My BEd or MEd is written clearly with university and year and marks", "- Education shows degree and intermediate and matric with marks and board and year", "- The CV fits the door I am applying to and runs two pages or less for school posts", "- My phone number and email are typed correctly and my photo is plain and professional", "- Nothing on this page is copied or invented and I can defend every line in an interview", "If all seven boxes tick true your CV is already ahead of most of the pile on the principal office table.", "And if building all this in Word feels like a fight you can build the same format free in our [free online CV maker](https://cvmakerpakistan.com/resume-builder) and print it to PDF without any sign up. Your data stays in your browser. Nothing is uploaded anywhere and nothing is stored on a server. For the base format behind every post you can also read the complete [CV format in Pakistan](https://cvmakerpakistan.com/cv-format-pakistan) guide and the [CV format for freshers in Pakistan](https://cvmakerpakistan.com/cv-for-freshers-pakistan) guide if you are starting your teaching career this year."] },
 { h: "Final Words for the Teachers Reading This", p: ["Teaching is the one profession where your work already has a public scoreboard. Your students carry your results into board halls every single year.", "The teacher from Multan did not become a better teacher in that one evening we spent on her CV. She became a readable one. Her six years were always there. They were just written in a language no principal could hear.", "Take one evening before the next hiring season opens. Rebuild your CV in the order this guide gives you. Then apply with a calm heart and walk into your demo lesson like the teacher your results already say you are.", "And one small request before you go. If this guide helped you then share it with one teacher friend who is hunting for a job this season. It takes ten seconds and it helps this free guide reach the teachers who need it most."] },
 { h: "A Short Note From the Writer", p: ["My name is Muhammad Kashif and I built the free CV maker on this site after watching good candidates around me lose interviews to bad formatting. Teacher CVs reach me in bundles every hiring season and almost every rule in this guide comes from one of those real pages and the quiet mistakes sitting on it. I am not a recruiter and I am not a principal. I am the person who fixes the document before the principal sees it and this guide is written from exactly that chair."] },
], faq: [{ q: "What is the correct CV format for a teacher in Pakistan?", a: "Start with your name and the teaching post you want and then a summary that names your subjects and your classes in the first lines. Follow with education including your BEd or MEd written clearly and then teaching experience written as results with board numbers wherever you honestly have them. Close with training and skills and languages and a references line." }, { q: "Should I include my BEd if it is still in progress?", a: "Yes and write it honestly as in progress with the university name and the expected completion year. Schools understand a running degree and many hire teachers who are completing their BEd alongside the job. What you must never do is write it as completed before your result is actually in your hand because schools do verify education documents." }, { q: "How long should a teacher CV be in Pakistan?", a: "School posts are served best by a tight CV of one to two pages with subjects and results kept near the top. College lecturer posts can honestly run longer because a subject degree record and research and publications belong there. Going beyond that almost always means old and irrelevant detail is sitting on the page where your results should be shining." }, { q: "Should a teacher CV have a photo in Pakistan?", a: "Private schools and government posts in Pakistan commonly expect a plain professional photo on the CV and teaching is a conservative field where presentation matters. Use a simple head and shoulders photo with a clean background and formal dress. Never use a wedding picture or a selfie or a photo taken at a family function whatever the post." }, { q: "How do I write board results on my teacher CV?", a: "Write them inside your experience lines with the class and the subject and the honest numbers attached. For example taught chemistry to classes nine and ten and raised the board pass rate of my sections from 78 percent to 93 percent in two years. A result written in that shape can be checked and trusted and it is the strongest line a teacher can own." }] },
];
export type Example = { slug: string; role: string; tag: string; summary: string; skills: string[]; bullets: string[]; tip: string; template?: TemplateId; faq?: { q: string; a: string }[] };
export const examples: Example[] = [
{ slug: "software-engineer", role: "Software Engineer", tag: "Show scale, reliability and ownership.", summary: "Software engineer with 5 years building scalable web services in TypeScript and Go, focused on reliability and clean APIs.", skills: ["TypeScript", "Go", "PostgreSQL", "AWS", "CI/CD"], bullets: ["Reduced API p95 latency by 40% by redesigning caching", "Led migration of 12 services to containers with zero downtime", "Mentored 3 junior engineers through code review"], tip: "Name the stack and quantify impact; recruiters search by technology.", faq: [{ q: "How long should a software engineer CV be?", a: "One page for under five years of experience and up to two pages for senior engineers. Keep only achievements that prove scale, reliability or ownership." }, { q: "What matters most on a software engineer CV?", a: "The exact stack you use, the systems you shipped and measurable results such as lower latency, higher uptime or reduced cost." }, { q: "Should I list every programming language I know?", a: "No. List languages you can actually work in and would defend in an interview. A short honest list beats a long padded one." }, { q: "How do I show impact without revealing company secrets?", a: "Use percentages and scale instead of confidential figures: latency cut by 40 percent, or an API serving millions of requests a day." }] },
{ slug: "frontend-developer", role: "Frontend Developer", tag: "Pair UI craft with performance and accessibility.", summary: "Frontend developer experienced in React and Next.js, building fast, accessible interfaces used by 200k monthly users.", skills: ["React", "Next.js", "TypeScript", "Tailwind CSS", "Accessibility"], bullets: ["Improved Largest Contentful Paint from 3.8s to 1.9s", "Built a design-system component library used by 4 teams", "Raised Lighthouse accessibility score from 72 to 98"], tip: "Link a portfolio or GitHub and mention Core Web Vitals results.", faq: [{ q: "What should a frontend developer CV highlight?", a: "Performance results, accessibility scores and interfaces real users use. Mention Core Web Vitals improvements and the component systems you built." }, { q: "Should I link my portfolio on my CV?", a: "Yes. Link one portfolio or GitHub profile with live projects. Recruiters trust a working demo more than a list of frameworks." }, { q: "How many frameworks should I list?", a: "List the frameworks you use confidently, such as React and Next.js, and skip ones you only tried once. Depth beats breadth." }, { q: "Does design skill matter on a frontend CV?", a: "Yes. Mention design systems, responsive layouts and accessibility work, because frontend roles sit between design and engineering." }] },
{ slug: "backend-developer", role: "Backend Developer", tag: "Highlight data, APIs and uptime.", summary: "Backend developer skilled in Node.js and Python, designing secure REST APIs and data pipelines.", skills: ["Node.js", "Python", "REST APIs", "PostgreSQL", "Docker"], bullets: ["Designed an order API handling 2M requests per day", "Cut database costs 25% by optimizing slow queries", "Added automated tests, raising coverage from 40% to 85%"], tip: "Include throughput, uptime and cost numbers where possible.", faq: [{ q: "What should a backend developer CV focus on?", a: "APIs you designed, data you handled and reliability you delivered. Throughput, uptime and cost savings are the numbers recruiters look for." }, { q: "Should I mention databases on my CV?", a: "Yes. Name the databases you use, such as PostgreSQL, and say what you did with them: schema design, slow-query fixes or cost cuts." }, { q: "How do I prove API experience?", a: "Describe one API end to end: its purpose, its traffic, how you secured it and how you tested it." }, { q: "Do certifications help a backend CV?", a: "They help when they match the stack, such as cloud or database certifications. Real shipped systems still matter more than certificates alone." }] },
{ slug: "full-stack-developer", role: "Full Stack Developer", tag: "Prove you can ship end to end.", summary: "Full stack developer delivering products from database to UI with React, Node.js and PostgreSQL.", skills: ["React", "Node.js", "TypeScript", "PostgreSQL", "Git"], bullets: ["Shipped a booking platform from concept to launch in 4 months", "Built admin dashboards that cut support tickets by 30%", "Set up CI that reduced release time from 2 hours to 15 minutes"], tip: "Describe one project you owned across the whole stack.", faq: [{ q: "What makes a full stack CV convincing?", a: "One project you owned from database to interface. Say what you built, which stack you used and what changed after launch." }, { q: "Should I list frontend and backend skills separately?", a: "Yes. Group them clearly so recruiters see both sides: interface tools in one place, server and database tools in another." }, { q: "How long should a full stack CV be?", a: "One page for early-career developers and up to two pages once you have shipped several products. Lead with the strongest product." }, { q: "Is a personal project worth including?", a: "Yes, if it is finished and usable. A deployed project with real users proves more than a tutorial clone." }] },
{ slug: "data-analyst", role: "Data Analyst", tag: "Turn analysis into decisions.", summary: "Data analyst who turns messy data into clear dashboards using SQL, Python and Power BI.", skills: ["SQL", "Python", "Excel", "Power BI", "Statistics"], bullets: ["Built churn dashboard that helped retain 8% more customers", "Automated weekly reporting, saving 6 hours per week", "Ran A/B tests that lifted conversion by 12%"], tip: "Always state the business decision your analysis influenced.", faq: [{ q: "What should a data analyst CV show?", a: "Decisions your analysis influenced, not just tools. State the dashboard, the finding and the business result, such as retained customers or saved hours." }, { q: "Which tools should I list as a data analyst?", a: "SQL and Excel first, then Python or Power BI if you use them. Match the posting: if it asks for Power BI, show Power BI work." }, { q: "How do I show SQL experience without a job?", a: "Use a course or personal project: describe the dataset, the queries you wrote and the answer you found." }, { q: "Do I need statistics on a data analyst CV?", a: "Yes. Mention the methods you use, such as A/B testing or regression, and one result each method produced." }] },
{ slug: "student", role: "Student", tag: "Lead with education, projects and activities.", summary: "Second-year computer science student seeking a summer internship, with project experience and strong teamwork.", skills: ["Python", "Java", "Teamwork", "Public Speaking", "Microsoft Office"], bullets: ["Built a campus event app used by 300 students", "Treasurer of the coding club, managing a 500 USD budget", "Tutored 10 classmates in introductory programming"], tip: "Put education first and include relevant coursework and clubs.", faq: [{ q: "What should a student CV lead with?", a: "Education first, then projects, internships and activities. Recruiters expect a student CV to prove potential, not years of experience." }, { q: "How long should a student CV be?", a: "One page. Keep it to education, two or three projects and skills you can actually demonstrate." }, { q: "Should I include part-time jobs on a student CV?", a: "Yes, if they show useful skills such as teamwork, cash handling or communication. One line each is enough." }, { q: "What if I have no internship yet?", a: "Use coursework projects, club roles and volunteer work. Something real with a result beats an empty experience section." }] },
{ slug: "fresh-graduate", role: "Fresh Graduate", tag: "Make internships and projects count as experience.", summary: "Recent business graduate with internship experience in marketing analytics, eager to start an entry-level role.", skills: ["Excel", "Google Analytics", "Communication", "Research", "Presentation"], bullets: ["Analyzed campaign data during a 3-month internship, finding 2 underperforming channels", "Graduated with a 3.7 GPA and dissertation on consumer behavior", "Organized a charity event raising 1,500 USD"], tip: "Use the Graduate template and keep it to one page.", faq: [{ q: "How does a fresh graduate CV differ from a student CV?", a: "It targets a real role: the summary names the job you want, and internships and projects are written as experience with results." }, { q: "Should a fresh graduate CV be one page?", a: "Yes. One page is the expectation. Cut hobbies and generic duties before cutting projects or internships." }, { q: "What if my GPA is low?", a: "Leave it off and lead with projects, internships and skills. A strong project says more than a weak number." }, { q: "Should I tailor my CV for each job?", a: "Yes. Mirror the posting's honest keywords in your skills and bullets. One generic CV sent everywhere performs worse than a tailored one." }] },
{ slug: "teacher", role: "Teacher", tag: "Show learning outcomes and classroom leadership.", summary: "Licensed primary school teacher with 6 years of experience improving literacy through differentiated instruction.", skills: ["Lesson Planning", "Classroom Management", "Differentiated Instruction", "Parent Communication", "Assessment"], bullets: ["Raised class reading scores by 18% in one academic year", "Designed a project-based unit adopted by the grade team", "Mentored 2 student teachers"], tip: "List licences and certifications clearly; use measurable student outcomes.", faq: [{ q: "What should a teacher CV highlight?", a: "Learning outcomes with numbers, such as reading scores improved by 18 percent, plus classroom leadership and licences." }, { q: "Should I list certifications on a teacher CV?", a: "Yes, clearly and with dates. Licences and subject certifications are often checked before shortlisting." }, { q: "How long should a teacher CV be?", a: "One page for early-career teachers and up to two pages with several years of results and leadership roles." }, { q: "Do I need a photo on a teacher CV?", a: "Not for most private-sector or international roles. Add one only if the advertisement asks for it." }] },
{ slug: "masters-application-cv", role: "Master's Application CV", tag: "A masters application CV that proves academic readiness: thesis, research skills and clear motivation for the programme.", summary: "Recent computer science graduate applying for a masters in data science, with a final-year thesis on predicting student performance, a 3-month university research internship and strong grades in mathematics, statistics and programming.", skills: ["Research Methods", "Academic Writing", "Python", "Statistics", "Data Analysis"], bullets: ["Completed a final-year thesis on student performance prediction, analysing survey data from 300 respondents with Python and regression models", "Worked as a research intern for 3 months, cleaning datasets, running basic statistical tests and preparing charts for a supervisor-led project", "Graduated with a 3.8 GPA, with top grades in statistics, databases and machine learning modules", "Presented thesis findings at a university research seminar attended by 60 students and faculty members"], tip: "Admissions tutors read for academic readiness, not work history. Put education first, name your thesis title and methods, list research skills honestly, and keep the CV to 2 pages. Attach transcripts and recommendation letters separately: the CV itself should prove you can handle graduate-level research.", template: "editorial", faq: [{ q: "How long should a masters application CV be?", a: "Usually two pages. One page feels thin for graduate applications, and more than two pages buries your academic record." }, { q: "What should I put first on a masters CV?", a: "Education, then thesis and research experience. Admissions tutors check academic readiness before anything else." }, { q: "Do I need a statement of purpose as well?", a: "Yes, most programmes ask for one separately. The CV proves your record; the statement explains your motivation and research fit." }, { q: "Should I include recommendation letters with my CV?", a: "No, submit them as the programme asks, usually directly from referees. The CV should name your referees only if asked." }] },
{ slug: "phd-application-cv", role: "PhD Application CV", tag: "A PhD application CV built around research output: publications, methods, lab skills and teaching.", summary: "Masters graduate in biotechnology applying for a PhD in molecular biology, with a co-authored conference paper, 12 months of laboratory research and two semesters as an undergraduate teaching assistant.", skills: ["Research Design", "Laboratory Methods", "Academic Writing", "SPSS", "Presentation"], bullets: ["Co-authored a conference paper on low-cost water testing, presented at a national research conference", "Ran laboratory experiments for 12 months, recording results, analysing data with SPSS and reporting findings to a supervisor-led team", "Assisted teaching in two undergraduate lab courses, guiding 40 students through practical sessions and marking lab reports", "Reviewed 25 published studies to identify the research gap behind the proposed PhD topic"], tip: "A PhD CV is judged on research fit. List publications and conference papers first, then methods and lab techniques, then teaching. Name the research area and the supervisor or group you want to join, and keep the CV focused: a long list of unrelated jobs weakens it. Contacting a potential supervisor before applying also strengthens the application.", template: "royal", faq: [{ q: "How is a PhD CV different from a job CV?", a: "It is longer and research-first: publications, methods, lab skills and teaching come before unrelated jobs." }, { q: "Should I contact a supervisor before applying?", a: "Yes. A short email with your research interest and CV often matters as much as the formal application." }, { q: "What if I have no publications yet?", a: "Show research experience instead: methods you used, data you handled and any conference presentation or thesis work." }, { q: "How long should a PhD CV be?", a: "Two to three pages is normal for research applicants, because publications and methods need space. Keep every line relevant to research." }] },
];
